A proto like this:

Generates C# code like this:

``` csharp
public const int 0FieldNumber = 1;
private bool has0;
private string 0_ = "";
public bool Has0 {
  get { return has0; }
}
public string 0 {
  get { return 0_; }
}
```

The `0FieldNumber`, `0` and `0_` field/property names are invalid.

It looks like the Java code generator creates invalid code for this too, so although this is a regression in the C# codegen compared with the protobuf-csharp-port generator, we might deem it a reasonable restriction - but it would probably make sense to prohibit it explicitly.

Sample proto:

```
syntax = "proto3";

package foo;

message A {
    int32 _A = 1;
}
```

Note that `A` and `_A` are the same after underscore stripping, so the deduplification code adds an underscore to the property name - which then collides with the field name:

```
private int A_;
public bool HasA_ {
  get { return hasA_; }
}
public int A_ {
  get { return A_; }
}
```

This was originally a problem in the previous C# code gen too:
https://github.com/jskeet/protobuf-csharp-port/issues/13

Looks like it was fixed by this change:
https://github.com/jskeet/protobuf-csharp-port/commit/c7b23c17979e93fb91be6e9a35ec0aef4a73704d

(That may not be directly applicable in the C++ codebase, but it's a good starting point.)

