I'm sorry I wasn't very specific.
I mean when protoc parses .proto file and generates language-specific files. One of such examples would be file with a single line, like this:

```
option csharp_namespace = "\U001FFFFF";
```

This will cause protoc to generate .cs file containing invalid character. While it's not much of a problem as it won't compile anyway, it could cause some harm in user-defined options or default values.
As another example, the following .proto:

```
syntax = "proto2";
option csharp_namespace = "δ";
import "google/protobuf/descriptor.proto";
extend google.protobuf.FieldOptions {
    optional string singleCharOnlyOption = 1354235 [default = "\U001FFFFF"];
}
```

will happily generate a field with given default value. When decoded in C#, it turns (in my tests) into four "replacement character" with code \uFFFD. 
At the same time, protoc will not compile values like \U00200000 with error "Expected eight hex digits up to 10ffff for \U escape sequence".
It also seems to allow surrogate pair values in \u and \U escapes, which it probably shouldn't.

