There are a couple of cases where the new arena code can produce blocks which are smaller than expected.

There is a relevant TODO in `Arena::NewBlock`:

```
if (n > size - kHeaderSize) {
  // TODO(sanjay): Check if n + kHeaderSize would overflow
  size = kHeaderSize + n;
}
```

In `Arena::AllocateAligned`, the alignment/padding computation can silently wrap around to zero:

```
// Align n to next multiple of 8 (from Hacker's Delight, Chapter 3.)
n = (n + 7) & -8;
```

Dear ProtoBuf Team

I am currently using MessageNano to exchange messages between two android.os.HandlerThread (Looper with a Handler and a MessageQueue).
So far I was able to keep the heap trashing to zero, except that now I have to use the toByteArray function in order to serialize the binary data.

In the background I already have a com.android.volley.toolbox.ByteArrayPool for the raw byte array, however the current implementation of MessageNano creates a new instance of CodedOutputByteBufferNano for each toByteArray call.

```
/**
 * Serialize to a byte array starting at offset through length. The
 * method getSerializedSize must have been called prior to calling
 * this method so the proper length is know.  If an attempt to
 * write more than length bytes OutOfSpaceException will be thrown
 * and if length bytes are not written then IllegalStateException
 * is thrown.
 */
public static final void toByteArray(MessageNano msg, byte[] data, int offset, int length) {
    try {
        final CodedOutputByteBufferNano output =
            CodedOutputByteBufferNano.newInstance(data, offset, length);
        msg.writeTo(output);
        output.checkNoSpaceLeft();
    } catch (IOException e) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Serializing to a byte array threw an IOException "
                + "(should never happen).", e);
    }
}
```

Wouldn't it be nice to have a heapless MessageNano.toByteArray?

