}
```

`protoc` adds an extra dot before "All", resulting in invalid Java. The workaround is to specify a package in `all.proto`.

I need to check what other platforms do, but in writing the JSON parser I've found that two messages which differ by oneof _case_ are considered equal, when the values in the oneof fields are the default values. Sample currently-failing test:

``` csharp
[Test]
public void Oneof_DefaultValuesNotEqual()
{
    var message1 = new TestAllTypes { OneofString = "" };
    var message2 = new TestAllTypes { OneofUint32 = 0 };
    Assert.AreEqual(TestAllTypes.OneofFieldOneofCase.OneofString, message1.OneofFieldCase);
    Assert.AreEqual(TestAllTypes.OneofFieldOneofCase.OneofUint32, message2.OneofFieldCase);
    Assert.AreNotEqual(message1, message2);
}
```

In `protobuf-java-util` from 3.0.0-beta-1, it is possible to write multiple objects in JSON format to an `Appendable` like this:

``` java
Printer jsonPrinter = JsonFormat.printer();
Writer writer = ...
jsonPrinter.appendTo(protoObject1, writer);
jsonPrinter.appendTo(protoObject2, writer);
```

However, it is impossible to read multiple objects in JSON format from a `Reader`.  I would like to be able to do something similar to this:

``` java
Parser jsonParser = JsonFormat.parser();
Reader reader = ...
jsonParser.merge(reader, protoObject1Builder);
jsonParser.merge(reader, protoObject2Builder);
```

This _does not work_ because the first call to `jsonParser.merge` constructs a `JsonReader` which fills an internal buffer (`JsonReader.buffer`) from data from the `reader`.  This advances the `reader` past the end of the JSON object being read.  After the merge is complete, the `JsonReader` is thrown away, which also throws away any unread data from its internal buffer.

In the second call to `jsonParser.merge`, a new `JsonReader` is constructed.  But since the `reader` has advanced too far, it is unable to read the next object.

This can easily be reproduced using a proto message with no fields.  When trying to read two instances of the message in JSON format (`{}{}`) from the same reader, the second merge will fail with an exception like this: `com.google.protobuf.InvalidProtocolBufferException: Expect message object but got: null`

