I've managed to implement a simple sample.

So, we have four proto files, each one contains one message

```
message MessageA {
    string id = 1;
}

message MessageB {
    MessageA a = 1;
}

message MessageC {
    MessageA a = 1;
}

message MessageD {
    MessageB b = 1;
    MessageC c = 2;
}
```

And when you execute this code:

```
final MessageA a = MessageA.newBuilder().setId("0").build();
final MessageB b = MessageB.newBuilder().setA(a).build();
final MessageC c = MessageC.newBuilder().setA(a).build();
final MessageD d = MessageD.newBuilder()
                .setB(b).setC(c).build();

final Descriptors.Descriptor descriptorD = d.getDescriptorForType();
final JsonFormat.TypeRegistry.Builder jsonFormatBuilder =
                JsonFormat.TypeRegistry.newBuilder();

jsonFormatBuilder.add(descriptorD).build();
```

you'll get the warning:
`WARNING: Type MessageA is added multiple times.`

It's happening because both MessageB and MessageC have dependencies to MessageA. When TypeRegistry accepts the descriptor, it calls addFile() method for it, which recursively calls addFile() for each dependency. As we have MessageA dependencies added twice, we get the warning

Each type is defined only once.

