}
```

As a result, building fails with following error

```
Undefined symbols for architecture x86_64:
  "_OBJC_IVAR_$_GPBDescriptor.oneofs_", referenced from:
      _HLWHelloRequest_ClearDataOneOfCase in Hello.pbobjc.o
ld: symbol(s) not found for architecture x86_64
clang: error: linker command failed with exit code 1 (use -v to see invocation)
```

My guess is [this line](https://github.com/google/protobuf/blob/c8a440dfb68074ff310e624928cd2dd61c101728/objectivec/GPBDescriptor_PackagePrivate.h#L140) is only accessible within framework (because `@package` directive), so when using dynamic framework it fails.

`heka_py` defines the following [protocol buffer](https://github.com/kalail/heka-py/blob/d975dc7d0f285aee86f8e74037391856d1c46790/protobuf/message.proto#L16-L25):

``` proto
message Field {
  enum ValueType {
    STRING  = 0;
    BYTES   = 1;
    INTEGER = 2;
    DOUBLE  = 3;
    BOOL    = 4;
  }
  required string       name            = 1;
  optional ValueType    value_type      = 2 [default = STRING];
  optional string       representation  = 3;
  repeated string       value_string    = 4;
  repeated bytes        value_bytes     = 5;
  repeated int64        value_integer   = 6 [packed=true];
  repeated double       value_double    = 7 [packed=true];
  repeated bool         value_bool      = 8 [packed=true];
}
```

Notably, this defines an `enum`, "`ValueType`" (the message here carries a value, which is one of several types; `value_type` encodes which type the message encodes)

The code thus inspects `value_type`, and uses it to determine, through [a mapping](https://github.com/kalail/heka-py/blob/d975dc7d0f285aee86f8e74037391856d1c46790/heka/message.py#L81-L86), which attribute on the message to inspect, i.e.,

``` python
_FIELD_TYPE_TO_ATTRIBUTE = {
    Field.INTEGER: 'value_integer',
    Field.DOUBLE: 'value_double',
    Field.BOOL: 'value_bool',
    Field.STRING: 'value_string',
}
```

Here, it uses `Field.INTEGER`, which is the value of the `INTEGER` enum. (`Field` here is a subclass of `google.protobuf.message.Message`, as created by the protobuf compiler.) This attribute _exists_ in `protobuf>=2,<3`, but does not exist in `protobuf==3.0.0b3`, breaking `heka_py`.

(I, as a consumer of `heka_py`, am forced to use `protobuf==3.0.0b3` as `protobuf` v2 only supports Python 2.)

<s>I find it a bit odd to access the enum values on the _Message_ class; one would imagine that if the message defined two separate enum types with corresponding names but different values, this would result in a name collision on the `Message` subclass. There exists `Field.ValueType` (but only in protobuf 2), but that doesn't have the `INTEGER` etc. attributes. (This was causing me to wonder if `heka_py` is using something part of the API that wasn't intended to be as exposed.)</s> No, this is documented. I have no idea what two enums with conflicting values would do.

I can't find — anywhere — `INTEGER` as an attribute. There's `Field.DESCRIPTOR.enum_values_by_name['INTEGER']`, but that seems incredibly unwieldy.

I've done some testing with protobuf version 3.0.0-beta3.1 with our existing internal codebase and found its slower in all benchmarking i've done (10-20% regression compared to version 2.6.1). We're still using proto2 and only changed the .proto file by adding:

```
syntax = "proto2";
option cc_enable_arenas = true;
```

Using arenas explicitly in the code seemed to not affecting timings in any meaningful way. Is google also seeing this?

