Tim,

Based on the discussion in https://github.com/google/protobuf/pull/1442#discussion_r72005149, how would you feel about a `swift_package_prefix` option that replaces the `Package_Subpackage_` part of `Package_Subpackage_Message` with a different prefix (similar to the way Objective-C works today)? It's not ideal, but it seems like the best option until Swift has some kind of proper namespacing support, and this would not require users to manually curate a potentially long list of their own typealiases.

For the second point, you say "The only exception (in Swift 3) is that a Swift extension cannot add protocol conformances". I don't believe that's the case, since that's one of the major features of extensions in Swift (I've personally used it a number of times). The following seems to work in both Swift 2 and Swift 3, from my own testing:

``` swift
struct SomeValue {
  var value: Double
}

extension SomeValue: FloatLiteralConvertible {
  init(floatLiteral value: Double) {
    self.init(value: value)
  }
}

let x: SomeValue = 5.0
x.value  // 5.0
```

Given that, the second option you're proposing shouldn't be necessary; extensions for well-known types can be added whether or not they add protocol conformances.

> how would you feel about a swift_package_prefix option that replaces the Package_Subpackage_ part of Package_Subpackage_Message with a different prefix (similar to the way Objective-C works today)?

That's exactly the behavior I'm proposing for swift_prefix.

To be specific, in the normal case, I expect that:

```
package foo.bar;
message Baz { }
```

becomes

```
struct Foo_Bar_Baz { ... }
```

I am proposing that:

```
package foo.bar;
option swift_prefix="My";
message Baz { }
```

generate

```
struct MyBaz { ... }
```

