The Java protocol buffers API allows clients to get and set field values using a Field Descriptor:

https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/reference/java/com/google/protobuf/GeneratedMessage.Builder#setField(com.google.protobuf.Descriptors.FieldDescriptor,%20java.lang.Object)

https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/reference/java/com/google/protobuf/GeneratedMessage#getField(com.google.protobuf.Descriptors.FieldDescriptor)

It would be extremely useful if this functionality were supported by the Objective C descriptor API. Here's a strawman API to demonstrate:

``` objectivec
@interface GPBMessage : NSObject
- (nullable id)fieldValueForDescriptor:(GPBFieldDescriptor *)descriptor;
- (void)setFieldValue:(id)fieldValue forDescriptor:(GPBFieldDescriptor *)descriptor;
- (void)clearFieldForDescriptor:(GPBFieldDescriptor *)descriptor;
@end
```

https://github.com/google/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/any.proto#L78-L82  mentions:

``` protobuf
// The pack methods provided by protobuf library will by default use
// 'type.googleapis.com/full.type.name' as the type URL and the unpack
// methods only use the fully qualified type name after the last '/'
// in the type URL, for example "foo.bar.com/x/y.z" will yield type
// name "y.z".
```

But then it also mentions: https://github.com/google/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/any.proto#L125-L126

``` protobuf
  // * An HTTP GET on the URL must yield a [google.protobuf.Type][]
  //   value in binary format, or produce an error.
```

Is this second part really required, or should we just drop it from the file/spec (and like the next bullet about fetching/caching them.  Seems like most implementations only plan on supporting what is baked into a given binary.  Report fetching support seems like it would be opening a vector that could cause problems for a binary - what if the fetched descriptor tries to register something already registered?  Are all runtimes hardened against that?

