Expanding on my last comment, I'm not sure I follow the need to use the `NSArray`/`NSDictionary` first. You don't actually need to know the capacity of the GPB* up front, they can grow as you add items. For example it would be more efficient to use code like:

```objective-c
int32_t v1 = ...;
int32_t v2 = ...;
GPBInt32Array *array = [GPBInt32Array array];
[array addValue:v1];
[array addValue:v2];
...
```

Than constructing an intermediate NSArray:

```objective-c
int32_t v1 = ...;
int32_t v2 = ...;
GPBInt32Array *array = [GPBInt32Array array];
[array addValuesFromNSArray:@[ @(v1), @(v2) ]];
...
```

This avoids the overhead of alloc/init two `NSNumber`s, generating code to make an `NSArray` out of those, calling your helper to extract the numbers and add them to the `GPBInt32Array`, and finally release/dealloc'ing all of the intermediate `NSNumber`s and the `NSArray`.  So the overhead in code size, memory, and runtime is non trivial.

It may be that for your particular project you have other needs for the `NSArray`, but with the overhead of this approach it probably isn't something we would provide in the main library as it isn't a pattern we'd want to encourage.

