@konsumer The CodeGeneratorRequest you get from protoc actually have the options. However, it may be unretrievable if you write your plugin using a language without proto2 support.

You can verify the options data following these steps:
  * First dump the original binary data you get from protoc. Don't parse it because parsing the data into a proto message will cause the options to be discarded right away and that's why your dumped JSON doesn't appear to have any options.
  * In a protobuf source tree, use the following command to convert the dumped data to a readable text format:
    ```bash
    cat [dumped data file] | protoc --decode google.protobuf.compiler.CodeGeneratorRequest -I. -Ipath/to/googleapis google/protobuf/compiler/plugin.proto [path to your own .proto file]
    ````

The resulted text output should have everything in the CodeGeneratorRequest. In your particular case, it should have a section like this:
```
  service {
    name: "Greeter"
    method {
      name: "sayHello"
      input_type: ".helloworld.GreeterRequest"
      output_type: ".helloworld.GreeterReply"
      options {
        [google.api.http] {
          post: "/v1/hi"
          body: "*"
        }
      }
    }
    method {
      name: "sayGoodbye"
      input_type: ".helloworld.GreeterRequest"
      output_type: ".helloworld.GreeterReply"
      options {
        [google.api.http] {
          post: "/v1/bye"
          body: "*"
        }
      }
    }
  }
```

The options data are represented using proto2 extensions, so if you are using a language without proto2 support, parsing the data into a proto message will render the options data unaccessible (they will be discarded as unknown fields). To preserve these options data, we will have to add additional APIs to keep and expose these options data. I remember there was some discussions around it but at the moment it hasn't be implemented in most proto3-only languages. @acozzette @haberman may know more about the current status of custom options support in Javascript.

There is also a planned proto3 change that introduces unknown fields back (i.e., unknown options data will no longer be discarded in proto3) and with that you should be able to access options data without adding other option specific APIs. It will take some time for this change to land though.

@xfxyjwf Actually we have support for proto2 and extensions for Javascript, so this should work.

@konsumer You should be able to access those custom options as extensions; here's an example of the extension syntax: https://github.com/google/protobuf/blob/40f3586412c2efa7a81ccb685e6f96e817996e36/js/binary/proto_test.js#L287
Note that those annotation options are just defined as an extension of `google.protobuf.MethodOptions`: https://github.com/googleapis/googleapis/blob/6ae2d424245deeb34cf73c4f7aba31f1079bcc40/google/api/annotations.proto#L28

