body:
As stated in the [encoding documentation](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/encoding#optional),

> Protocol buffer parsers must be able to parse repeated fields that were compiled as packed as if they were not packed, and vice versa. This permits adding [packed=true] to existing fields in a forward- and backward-compatible way.

This does not seem to be true for the 3.1.0 C++ protobuf parser. Generated code for a repeated field with `[packed=false]` cannot parse a message serialized by generated code with `[packed=true]` if the field number is greater than 2047. (Incidentally, this is the largest field number for which the varint-encoded (field number, wire type) key is at most 2 bytes long.)

Reproduction case:

```
// test.proto
syntax = "proto2";
package test;

message Packed {
  repeated int32 a = 2048 [packed=true];
}

message NotPacked {
  repeated int32 a = 2048;
}
```

```
// test.cc
#include "test.pb.h"
#include <gtest/gtest.h>
#include <string>

TEST(Test, Packed2048) {
  test::Packed packed;
  packed.add_a(1234);
  std::string buffer;
  packed.SerializeToString(&buffer);
  
  test::NotPacked not_packed;
  not_packed.ParseFromString(buffer);
  EXPECT_EQ(1, not_packed.a_size()); // Actual: 0
}
```

The test passes if any other combination of packed/non-packed encodings is used for the two messages, or if the field number is changed to 2047 or less.

Update: after a little more testing, this only seems to apply to varint-encoded packed fields, i.e. changing the field types to `fixed32` above also makes the test pass. Also, I'm using 3.1.0, not 3.0.1 as I erroneously stated.

