or its functional equivalent (and then using `proto_string` instead of `string`, of course).

As it is now, the open-ended/"uninitialized" type identifier used in those places leaves things to chance: with luck, it will point to the correct type, or otherwise it will start summoning typesystem-level nasal demons just like conventional uninitialized variables do (which is exactly what happened in this issue report). Surely this is an actual valid issue that deserves to be fixed.

I would also like to point out that "std::string" is used all over the place in protobuf, so the special treatment of the two lines in question sounds quite inconsistent from the outside point of view (it might make sense from the internal codebase' point of view, but we're talking about a project published specifically to be used be a wider audience).

