Using `google-protobuf@3.2.0`:

Assume the following protobuf definition:

```
message Foo {
  string some_value = 1;
}
```

Using a console, you can note the following behavior:

```javascript
> var m = new Foo();
...
> m.setSomeValue(123);
undefined
> m.getSomeValue();  // '123' expected
123
```

It turns out that the protobuf message is storing something typed as a string as an integer when an integer is passed. This also results in incorrect serialization behavior. For instance:

```javascript
> var a = new Foo();
...
> var b = new Foo();
...
> a.setSomeValue(123);
undefined
> b.setSomeValue('123');
undefined
> a.serializeBinary() == b.serializeBinary()
false
```

