            {
                input?.Dispose(); // <-- Note conditional Dispose() since input may be null
            }
        }
```

Actually this is still an issue in a different circumstance.
The following code throws a `NullReferenceException` due to the stream being implicitly `null`;

     var foo = new byte[] { 1, 2 };
     using (var input = new CodedInputStream(foo))
     {
     } // Throws here (since Dispose sees that leaveopen is false so tries to free a null stream)

Arguably the caller should know that `using` is not necessary here, but that feels like a leaky abstraction. 
The `using` idiom should do the right thing regardless.

A simple fix would be to set `leaveOpen = true;` for non-stream (buffer only) constructors.

@xfxyjwf Here's a particular use case that we have ran into:

```
message Test {
    google.protobuf.StringValue name = 1;
}
```

A name value of '' is not treated the same way as a name value of null for our particular use case. In those cases, it was recommended that we use `google.protobuf.StringValue` instead of `string` to be able to differentiate between the null and 0 value cases. I think the JSON serialization output (when including_default_value_fields) is set should also be consistent in this case instead of omitting the field.

Output should be serialized to: `{"name": null}`, instead of `{}` when including_default_value_fields is set to True.

I'm only familiar w/ the Go implementation, you're right, it does by default omit zero/default value fields, but when the EmitDefaults option is set to true, the zero/default value fields are emitted.

For Python, when including_default_value_fields is set to True, json_format currently emits the default values for the primitive types, so I think it's more consistent to emit the equivalent "default" values for the Wrapper types (and by extension singular message types) as null.

Agreed that input deserialization to protobuf objects should treat both the undefined as well as null case as default values, which I believe it currently already does.

