I'm using [protodot](https://github.com/seamia/protodot) to generate structure graph in .dot format and [protoc-gen-doc](https://github.com/pseudomuto/protoc-gen-doc) for documentation. It will be very useful to visualize alias types dependencies at the graph and alias-type-links inside md/html/pdf documentation.

Currently the only hack is to create wrapper type:

    message my_entity_id_type {
        string id = 1;
    }

I would like any `typealias my_entity_id_type = string;` instead

> For those curious why this is not a very simply thing to add. aliases for messages interacts with parsing of Any fields and so it needs to be designed carefully to ensure long term stability.

@fowles how come? A type alias should not have any effect on `Any` since it still is the original type. Adding support for type aliases really should just be a pre-processing step that resolves each level of aliasing down to the original type while merging metadata together as needed to finally replace any usage with the actual type and all the metadata.

```protobuf
package example;

type Uuid = string [ (validate.rules).string = { uuid: true } ];

type A = string [ (custom).x = true, (custom).y = true ];

type B = A [ (custom).x = false, (custom).z = true ];

message M {
  Uuid id = 1;
  A a = 2;
  B b = 3;
}
```

We turn this into the following before we hand it out to any protoc plugin:

```protobuf
package example;

message M {
  string id = 1 [
    alias = "example.Uuid",
    (validate.rules).string = { uuid: true }
  ];

  string a = 2 [
    alias = "example.A",
    (custom).x = true,
    (custom).y = true
  ];

  string b = 3 [
    alias = "example.B",
    (custom).x = false,
    (custom).y = true,
    (custom).z = true
  ];
}
```

Basically the alias is gone entirely. Plugins that care about them can look at the metadata and react to it on a per field basis. The aliases themselves are not reflectible or accessible in any way. This really covers like 99% of all use cases. The only thing that is not possible is that a protoc plugin alters an alias, but I would not see this as a blocker to achieve a minimally invasive implementation that can be rolled out without fear.

"Type alias" is a somewhat overloaded term; the feature we have contemplated internally is a *message* type alias:

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

message Foo {}

// Hypothetical syntax.
//
// This would actually generate a type alias in each target language,
// so that both names are considered equivalent. 
message Bar = Foo;

message Baz {
  Bar bar = 1;
}
```

The motivation of message aliases would be to facilitate renaming of messages while allow clients to gradually migrate from the old name to the new name, very similar to the rationale given in [this Go proposal](https://go.googlesource.com/proposal/+/master/design/18130-type-alias.md):

> The primary motivation is to enable gradual code repair during large-scale refactorings, in particular moving a type from one package to another in such a way that code referring to the old name interoperates with code referring to the new name. 

Message aliases do end up causing problems for `Any`, since message names are embedded into wire payloads for `Any` messages, so changing the message name is a wire-breaking change.

The version of type aliases given on this thread, where the alias is a mere substitution of the field type (and custom options), would not have any problems with `Any`, I agree. But it also has not been as frequent of a feature request, and therefore has a less clear benefit to the relatively large amount of complexity it would introduce.

An alias is something that will allow me not to rewrite the same lines a million times and replace plugin descriptions in these lines instantly everywhere and at once

    string Email = 1 [
        json_name = "email",
        (grpc.gateway.protoc_gen_openapiv2.options.openapiv2_field) = {
            description: "User email"
            example: "testuser@example.ru"
        },
        (validate.rules).string = {
            email: true
            max_len: 1024
            ignore_empty: false
        }
    ];

and the attached message will be a nested field, for example if I use "message email" it looks like email.email

