This actually looks like a compiler bug. When calling the function the original type of message_value -
 ```google::protobuf::MessageLite* const``` should be ```google::protobuf::MessageLite*``` due to the copy in function call. The compiler seems to still treat the parameter type as the original const type, which is a bug. I guess it only happens when the compiler is dealing with the implicit const added in const functions.

Could you please file a bug to the IBM compiler? Here's the simple code to reproduce the issue:

```c++
template<typename Arg>
void F(Arg* arg) {}

struct S {
  void ConstFunction() const {
    F(value_);
  }

  int* value_;
};
```
When passing the value_ into a function, the const qualifier (added implicitly due to in a const function) should be removed in template matching, as it's a value copy in function call. The compiler used the ```int* const``` to match types.

