The C++ API generated by protoc (latest release 3.4.1) for repeated message fields seems partially broken.

Given definitions:

message Foo {
  optional string name = 1;
}

message Bar {
  repeated Foo foo = 1;
}

The API provides two alternates for iterating over repeated embedded message fields:

1) Index-based : OK

const Foo& Bar::foo(int index) const; // const method returns const reference

Foo* Bar::mutable_foo(int index); // non-const method returns non-const pointer for mutability

2) "STL-like iterator"-based : seems broken

RepeatedPtrField< Foo >& Bar::foo() const;

RepeatedPtrField< Foo >* Bar::mutable_foo();

This second STL-like API has the following issues:

a) foo(), unlike foo(int), behaves as a collection of non-const Foo despite being a const interface

b) mutable_foo() behaves as a collection of Foo rather than Foo*, and therefore fails to provide a mutable interface
		 
c) mutable_foo() oddly returns a pointer to collection rather than a collection itself

These issues manifest as follows:

for (auto foo : bar.foo()) {
  foo.set_name("fred"); // wrongly allowed, since non-const, but ineffectual anyway since value vs reference
}

for (auto foo : *bar.mutable_foo()) { // need to dereference collection !
  foo.set_name("fred"); // ineffectual since value vs expected pointer as returned by mutable_foo(int)
}

It seems the STL-like API should really be:

RepeatedPtrField< const Foo & > Bar::foo() const;

RepeatedPtrField< Foo * > Bar::mutable_foo();

Which would then support:

for (auto foo : bar.foo()) {
  foo.set_name("fred"); // compilation error - attempt to modify const object
}

for (auto foo_ptr : bar.mutable_foo()) {
  foo_ptr->set_name("fred"); // successfully modified via pointer - mutability achieved!
}

