According to the Spring JSON deserializer `org.springframework.http.converter.protobuf.ProtobufJsonFormatHttpMessageConverter`, the `oneof` members are directly embedded into the JSON as if the surrounding `oneof` doesn't exist.

E.g.
```
message A {
  int32 age = 1;
}
message {
  string id = 1;
  oneof SomeThing {
    A thingA = 2;
    string thingB = 3;
  }
}
```

Will become:
```
{ "id": "hello", "thingA": { "age": 30 } }
```

or
```
{ "id": "hello", "thingB": "walrus" }
```

> Can we please have this explicitly stated in the spec? It would help to settle a lot of bike-shedding debates on my team over how JSON should be formatted.

Since this issue was last discussed, I noticed that there's a new website containing the protobuf documentation. It doesn't give an explicit example of `oneof` being serialized into JSON, however, it does give enough information to infer how it behaves.

https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/proto3/#oneof

> Oneof fields are like regular fields except all the fields in a oneof share memory, and at most one field can be set at the same time.
> Move fields into or out of a oneof: You may lose some of your information (some fields will be cleared) after the message is serialized and parsed. However, you can safely move a single field into a new oneof and may be able to move multiple fields if it is known that only one is ever set. See [Updating A Message Type](https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/proto3/#updating) for further details.

The fact that an existing field can safely be moved into a `oneof` without creating parsing errors implies that it must have the same JSON representation, e.g. the following two messages must have the same JSON format:

```
message A {
  string a = 1;
  int32 b = 2;
}

message B {
  string a = 1;
  oneof test_oneof {
    int32 b = 2;
  }
}
```

