The `normalize(string path)` function in `io_win32.cc` relies on undefined behavior in C++03. The function loops through `path`, parsing the segments along the way. The loop breaks out when `path[i] == '\0'`. For example, see [line 163](https://github.com/jquesnelle/protobuf/blob/9021f623e1420f513268a01a5ad43a23618a84ba/src/google/protobuf/stubs/io_win32.cc#L163). `path` is a mutable `std::string`. According to C++03, `operator[]` with `size()` as the parameter (i.e. accessing where the null terminator is) only allowed for the `const` version of `operator[]`. 

[C++03](http://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2005/n1905.pdf), page 638:
>const_reference operator []( size_type pos ) const ;
>reference operator []( size_type pos );
> Returns: If pos < size() , returns *(begin() + pos ) . Otherwise, if pos == size() , the const version
>returns charT() . Otherwise, the behavior is undefined.

Since `path` is a mutable string, the compiler chooses the non-const version of `operator[]`, which leads to undefined behavior. I discovered this because Visual Studio 2010 explicitly checks for this condition if `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL != 0` and asserts, which is case when making debug builds.

Note: [C++11](http://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2013/n3690.pdf) changed the wording of this requirement. Page 651:

>const_reference operator[](size_type pos) const;
>reference operator[](size_type pos);
>Requires: pos <= size() .
>Returns: *(begin() + pos) if pos < size() . Otherwise, returns a reference to an object of type
charT with value charT() , where modifying the object leads to undefined behavior.

