I was playing around with the parser to attempt a fix for #2128 but I ran into a few conflicts with the unittest_custom_options.proto unit test. It looks like my fix makes some assumptions from the language specification that are violated by the unit test in question. Here are a few sample lines:

```
option (.protobuf_unittest.complex_opt1).(protobuf_unittest.corge).qux = 876;
option (protobuf_unittest.complex_opt1).(.protobuf_unittest.quux) = 324;
option (complex_opt2).(grault) = 654;
```

Here's the relevant portions of the specification:

```
ident = letter { letter | decimalDigit | "_" }
fullIdent = ident { "." ident }
option = "option" optionName  "=" constant ";"
optionName = ( ident | "(" fullIdent ")" ) { "." ident }
```

There are two separate violations. First, in the first example, the optionName spec suggests any term in parentheses must be a fullIdent, but it starts with a dot which the spec says is illegal for a fullIdent. This seems like it should instead result in an error. Second, the spec also says only the first fullIdent can be surrounded by parentheses, but the unit test has multiple fullIdent terms surrounded by parentheses. However, to me this makes sense for an optionName with nested options, and I feel it should be instead added to the specification. I wrote a revised spec definition of what I mean below. Any thoughts on these?

```
optionIdent = ( ident | "(" fullIdent ")" )
optionName = optionIdent { "." optionIdent }
```

