To my understanding, one of the major goals between proto2 and proto3 is to keep the on-the-wire message content compatibility. This is largely achieved, however there is a problem related to interoperability between systems that are using implementation derived from proto2 definition communicating with such using proto3.

It is related to how the Protobuf.Tools (at least for c#) generate the serialization code for scalar fields that are currently holding the default value for their respective scalar type.
For example, imagine a .proto2 definition for a message like:

message Property {
	required	uint32		id	 = 1;
	optional	string		name= 2;
	required	float			val 	 = 3;
}

and its equivalent proto3 definition:

message Property {
		uint32		id	 = 1;
		string		name = 2;
		float			val	  = 3;
}

When a proto3 implementation is generating a message of type "Property" that has a value of 0 (float) for the field "val", the on-the-wire message is generated such that it is lacking the field completely. This breaks proto2 implementations, which expect to always find a field carrying the value for the "val" field.

To overcome such problems, I need to manually edit the Protobuf.Tool generated implementation and make it serialize the field, even when it currently contains its default value of 0.

I would suggest to add a field option for the .proto3 file - for example:

message Property {
		uint32		id		= 1;
		string		name	= 2;
		float			val		= 3  **[always_serialize=true];**
}

The always_serialize field option would make the Protobuf.Tool generate the serialization, size computation, hashing, comparison and cloning implementation ignoring if the value is the default one - always generating the field on protocol level.

Protobuf should accept strings for Enum properties and return strings from Enum Constants. For example, [`PublicKeyCertificate::setFormat`](https://github.com/googleapis/proto-client-php/blob/c2d9d662c344db31ee793d1ad3c03827d225ee48/src/Google/Cloud/Iot/V1/PublicKeyCertificate.php#L60) should accept the strings `'unspecified'`, `'RSA_X509_PEM'`, `'ES256_PEM'`, etc, instead of requiring integers, e.g. `PublicKeyFormat::RSA_X509_PEM`:

```php
// current
$publicKey = (new PublicKeyCredential())
    ->setFormat(PublicKeyFormat::RSA_X509_PEM)
    ->setKey(file_get_contents($certificateFile));

// new
$publicKey = (new PublicKeyCredential())
    ->setFormat('RSA_X509_PEM')
    ->setKey(file_get_contents($certificateFile));
```

Similarly, for getters, we should provide an additional method (such as `getFormatString`) which returns the string representation of the constant:

```php
// current
$formats = [
    PublicKeyFormat::UNSPECIFIED_PUBLIC_KEY_FORMAT => 'unspecified',
    PublicKeyFormat::RSA_X509_PEM => 'RSA_X509_PEM',
    PublicKeyFormat::ES256_PEM => 'ES256_PEM',
    PublicKeyFormat::RSA_PEM => 'RSA_PEM',
    PublicKeyFormat::ES256_X509_PEM => 'ES256_X509_PEM',
];
foreach ($device->getCredentials() as $credential) {
    echo 'Format is ' . $formats[$credential->getPublicKey()->getFormat()];
}

// new
foreach ($device->getCredentials() as $credential) {
    echo 'Format is ' . $credential->getPublicKey()->getFormatString();
}
```

