Version: v3.5.1
OS: Windows 7 64-bit
Runtime: C++

**In short:** The functions in `io_win32.cc` misinterpret paths like `C:\foo\..\..` as being `C:\` rather than an invalid path. Similarly `C:\foo\..\..\..\bar` will be misinterpreted as `C:\bar`. One concrete bug resulting from this is that the unit test program will loop forever if run from the wrong working directory. But I suspect there may be other implications.

**More details:** The functions in `io_win32.cc` mostly (all?) call the internal `as_windows_path()` function, which in turn calls `normalize()`. This works by breaking the path apart into a vector of path segments, then joining them back together again. The segment splitting code contains this code section:

    if (segment == dotdot) {
      if (!segments.empty() &&
          (!has_drive_letter(segments[0].c_str()) || segments.size() > 1)) {
        segments.pop_back();
      }
    }

Note that if the condition of the inner clause is false then the segment is just discarded. I would argue that the function should immediately `return false` at this point, indicating an invalid path.

**Observable(ish) consequence:** Here is one effect this has, which is how I discovered the problem (while researching #4925): The unit tests require that they are run in a directory where `src/` contains the protobuf source code; in particular `src/google/protobuf` should exist. It is acceptable to be in a child directory instead, as `../src/` and `../../src/` etc. are also checked. However, if run from the wrong directory, this check just loops forever in `googletest.cc`:

    while (!File::Exists(prefix + "/src/google/protobuf")) {
      if (!File::Exists(prefix)) {
        GOOGLE_LOG(FATAL)
          << "Could not find protobuf source code.  Please run tests from "
             "somewhere within the protobuf source package.";
      }
      prefix += "/..";
    }

This loop assumes that `File::Exists(prefix)` will `return false` for invalid paths such as `C:\..` in order to terminate the loop. But `File::Exists(prefix)` calls `access()`, which calls `normalize()`, so it ends up checking whether `C:\` exists, which of course it does. After an hour in this loop, `prefix` was 1.4 million characters long :-)

