**What language does this apply to?**
proto3

**Describe the problem you are trying to solve.**
Oneof has several issues:
- "None of" is not 'safe' to use as there is no way to distinguish between no value set, and an 'unknown' value set. For example, if a new field was added to a oneof, code that lacks knowledge of that new field will see no value set in the oneof (and an unknown field value). In contrast a normal proto3 enum will show an 'unrecognized' value set, in a similar situation.
- Repeated and map fields not supported in oneof. I suspect this is due to the fact there is no encoded form of 'set, but empty' for these types of fields. 

**Describe the solution you'd like**
The ability to assign a field number to the oneof field:

    message Foo {
      oneof value_type = 1 {
        map<string, Bar> map_value = 2;
        repeated<Bar> list_value = 3;
      }
    }

In this case, 'value_type' would be a full fledged enum field, and this message would be ~equivalent to:

    message Foo {
      enum ValueTypeCase {
        // (BTW I regret making the standard for OnePlatform UNSPECIFIED, given that oneof uses NOT_SET).
        VALUE_TYPE_NOT_SET = 0;
        kMapValue = 2;
        kListValue = 3;
      }

      ValueTypeCase value_type = 1;
      map<string,  Bar> map_value = 2;
      repeated<Bar> list_value = 3;
    }
  
**Describe alternatives you've considered**
The current workarounds are:
For the first problem (no safe way to do 'none of'), you have to add a special oneof value to represent 'none of', and reject messages that don't have a value in the oneof set. Most often `google.protobuf.NullValue` is used for this (which is a bit awkward in code gen). This is actually why the `NullValue` enum was added in the first place. An enum with a single value is the only way to have a binary option in a oneof (vs trinary which is what you get with a bool in a oneof). Every usage of `NullValue` in google apis, is to solve this problem:
https://github.com/googleapis/googleapis/search?q=google.protobuf.NullValue&unscoped_q=google.protobuf.NullValue

For the second issue (no support for repeated/map fields), you have to wrap such fields in an additional message before including them in the oneof. There are also many such examples in the google apis protos:
https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/struct.proto#L77
https://github.com/googleapis/googleapis/blob/master/google/firestore/v1beta1/document.proto#L131
https://github.com/googleapis/googleapis/blob/master/google/api/expr/v1beta1/value.proto#L64

**Additional context**

**What language does this apply to?**
proto3

**Describe the problem you are trying to solve.**
proto3 requires that a zero-value be present in all enums. This zero-value is almost always ENUM_NAME_UNSPECIFIED as specified in the [google style guide](https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/design_patterns#enum_default_value) (btw, sorry I picked UNSPECIFIED instead of NOT_SET, which is what is used for the oneof case enum, though I was trying to be consistent with UNRECOGNIZED as described below).

**Describe the solution you'd like**
Just as the special enum value, UNRECOGNIZED, is created in closed enum languages, the zero value should be auto generated when not specified. For example:

    enum Foo {
      BAR = 1;
    }

in java could generate:

    enum Foo {
      UNSPECIFIED(0),
      BAR(1),
      UNRECOGNIZED(-1)
    }

in c++ it could generate:

    enum Foo {
      FOO_UNSPECIFIED = 0,
      BAR = 1,
      // c++ supports open enums, so no unrecognized value needed.
    };

If a zero value is specified (currently required by proto3), code gen is unchanged. Such a value supposed to be an idiomatic version of 'UNSPECIFIED', for example'OK=0;' in code.proto represents that lack of an error. 

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
Live with *_UNSPECIFIED in almost every enum forever.

