According to the [specs](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/reference/csharp-generated#oneof), this behaviour is correct. However, it is not useful.

> If a property is the current oneof "case", fetching that property will return the value set for that property. Otherwise, fetching the property will return the default value for the property's type - only one member of a oneof can be set at a time.

I argue that returning a default value and returning that the value has not been set, is not the same. In this simple case, if both `bar` and `baz` return false,  I am unable to tell which one of them was explicitly set to sale, and which one is not set. I think this contradicts with what `oneof` means.

