I just realized that you can do this:
```
if (request.FooBarCase == FooBarCase.None) 
{
    // .. none is set
}
```

I only understood this after reading the [Java documentation](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/reference/java-generated#oneof).

I think this paragraph is misleading.
> If a property is the current oneof "case", fetching that property will return the value set for that property. Otherwise, fetching the property will return the default value for the property's type - only one member of a oneof can be set at a time.

Although true, it gave me the impression that oneof didn't work. 

Java's paragraph made me understand it much better
> OneofNameCase getOneofNameCase(): Returns the enum indicating which field is set. Returns ONEOFNAME_NOT_SET if none of them is set.

