I am currently using Protocol Buffers in a C++ client/server application to send commands to a server and retrieve complex calculated results. Both the client and the server talk to other libraries that generate and receive the information stored in the protocol buffers. These libraries are used in other contexts as well which is why I would like to limit protobuf dependency to the client and the server. Unfortunately, this means that I can't use the generated classes in the client's or the server's interface. Right now I have to write structs or classes that contain all the information the client needs and manually translate them to the protobuf-generated format.

Consider the following simple example:
```
message Example {
  double x = 1;
}
```
This currently generates a C++ class that looks somewhat like this:
```
#include "google/protobuf/[...].h"
class Example {
  double get_x();
  void set_x(double value);
  bool ParseFromString(const string& data);
  bool SerializeToString(string* output) const;
};
```
In order to use this class without a dependency on the protobuf library I would like a solution that looks something like this:
```
class Example {
  double get_x();
  void set_x(double value);
};

#include "google/protobuf/[...].h
class ExampleSerializer {
  Example ParseFromString(const string& data);
  bool SerializeToString(Example* input, string* output) const;
};
```
This would allow me to use the Example class (or struct) independently - for example, to pass an instance of it to the client via its interface and then serialize it before sending. Is that possible somehow?

