Thanks Adam!
The solution I came up with is a [small python script](https://github.com/mmoeckel/cdm_protobuf/blob/master/proto2h.py) that takes a .proto file and generates a protobuf-independent C++ header containing the same data and an adapter class that converts between it and the protobuf-generated types. For example,

```
package CDM;
enum CovarianceMethod
{
    DEFAULT = 0;
    CALCULATED = 1;
}
```
in the .proto file becomes
```
namespace CDM_C {
enum CovarianceMethod
{
    DEFAULT = 0,
    CALCULATED = 1
};
}
```
in the independent C++ header and
```
static inline CDM_C::CovarianceMethod CovarianceMethod(const CDM::CovarianceMethod in)
{
    if (in == CDM::DEFAULT) return CDM_C::DEFAULT;
    if (in == CDM::CALCULATED) return CDM_C::CALCULATED;
    return CDM_C::DEFAULT;
}
static inline CDM::CovarianceMethod CovarianceMethod(const CDM_C::CovarianceMethod in)
{
    if (in == CDM_C::DEFAULT) return CDM::DEFAULT;
    if (in == CDM_C::CALCULATED) return CDM::CALCULATED;
    return CDM::DEFAULT;
}
```
in the adapter class. This is far from complete and only covers the small subset of the protobuf specification that I am currently using myself but maybe it's useful to someone who has the same problem.

