**Describe the problem you are trying to solve.**

Representing time is a fairly common pattern, so being able to use a built-in protobuf types is desirable for us to avoid inventing our own (if possible). Today, there exists the `Duration` and `Timestamp` types, so building on top of those and introducing additional time types could be useful.

Some services we have have built their own `Interval` types similar to the ones mentioned below. This means that each type is essentially the same, but require different imports and generation techniques. This can be especially annoying when dealing with multiple services that have identical `Interval` definitions but they are generated with different `package`s.

**Describe the solution you'd like**

A built-in protobuf type that might look like:

```
import "google/protobuf/timestamp.proto";

message Interval {
    google.protobuf.Timestamp start_inclusive = 1;
    google.protobuf.Timestamp end_exclusive = 2;
}
```

or

```
import "google/protobuf/timestamp.proto";

message Interval {
    google.protobuf.Timestamp start = 1;
    google.protobuf.Timestamp end = 2;
    bool is_end_inclusive = 3;
}
```

Or

```
import "google/protobuf/duration.proto";
import "google/protobuf/timestamp.proto";

message Interval {
    google.protobuf.Timestamp start = 1;
    google.protobuf.Duration duration = 2;
}
```

(I've seen a few different versions here)

**Describe alternatives you've considered**

We have been considering implementing our own common protobuf types package but that might introduce more overhead to users.

**Additional context**

