Sadly that does not help because the interface does not expose functions like `toBuilder` which returns the builder of the right type. Note that you don’t need experience in Kotlin to benefit from a proper relationship. The main reason why those generated interfaces you mention are not useful is because they do not allow us to target all messages with their corresponding builders, only specific ones.

Almost everything that Kotlin can do can be done in Java too, it usually just requires more typing. Maybe the following example which is basically equivalent to the Kotlin code above helps:

```java
import org.jetbrains.annotations.NotNull;

import java.util.function.Consumer;

interface Message<Self extends Message<Self, B>, B extends Message.Builder<Self, B>> {
    @NotNull B toBuilder();

    interface Builder<M extends Message<M, Self>, Self extends Builder<M, Self>> {
        @NotNull M build();
    }
}

final class Timestamp implements Message<Timestamp, Timestamp.Builder> {
    @Override
    public @NotNull Builder toBuilder() {
        return new Builder();
    }

    final static class Builder implements Message.Builder<Timestamp, Builder> {
        @NotNull Builder setSeconds(final long seconds) {
            return this;
        }
        
        @NotNull Builder setNanos(final int nanos) {
            return this;
        }
        
        @Override
        public @NotNull Timestamp build() {
            return new Timestamp();
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    static <M extends Message<M, B>, B extends Message.Builder<M, B>> @NotNull M copy(
        final @NotNull M message,
        final @NotNull Consumer<@NotNull B> action
    ) {
        final B builder = message.toBuilder();
        action.accept(builder);
        return builder.build();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final Timestamp ts1 = new Timestamp();

        final Timestamp ts2 = copy(ts1, builder -> {
            builder.setSeconds(1);
            builder.setNanos(1);
        });
    }
}
```

Of course, the functionality is not as useful in Java as it is in Kotlin, but it should clarify what this request is about. Basically we create a true relationship between a message and its builder. Note that this also enables us to add true covariant return methods to parents (with a safe unchecked cast):

```java
@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
interface Message<Self extends Message<Self, B>, B extends Message.Builder<Self, B>> {
    default @NotNull Self covariance() {
        return (Self) this;
    }

    interface Builder<M extends Message<M, Self>, Self extends Builder<M, Self>> {
        default @NotNull Self covariance() {
            return (Self) this;
        }
    }
}
```

OK, just got what you mean here.

I think changing interface directly is some kind of hard since it's a breaking change. Maybe it can be solved by adding a new class layer between generated class and GeneratedMessageV3 but it also need to regenerate all the protos to make it work.

@haon4 for acknowledgement, do you have any ideas here?

