On such platforms C 'long' (which [Python used as regular 'int' in 2.x](https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0237/)) is likely to be 32-bit, like C 'int'. 4636878028842991616 is too big to be a 32-bit integer - you need a 'long long', Python 2.7's 'long int'.

If such a large value is being round-tripped or interpreted through a 'long' that is only 32-bits, that might explain the issue. Especially since the least significant bits of the above integer are all zero. I could see it just grabbing the 32 bits that were 0, resulting in the `12: 0`. 

I don't have *any* experience with protobuf, or for that matter Python's interaction with native code, so take this with a huge grain of salt, but after wandering around for a few hours I found [this code](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/python/google/protobuf/pyext/unknown_fields.cc#L272):
