Currently all C# messages are generated without constructors. If you want to initialize a message, you can use object initialization pattern in C#. 

What I'm suggesting is an option to generate constructors for messages.
Take a look at this message (It's from a shooter game):
```
syntax = "proto3";
package ProtoMessages;

message Vec3
{
	float x = 1;
	float y = 2;
	float z = 3;
}

message Raycast
{
    Vec3 origin = 1;
    Vec3 hitPoint = 2;
    int32 hitPlayerID = 5;
    float interpolationTime = 6;
}
```

Currently you have to initialize this like this:
```
var raycast = new Raycast
{
    Origin = new Vec3 { X = 10, Y = 20, Z = 30 },
    HitPoint = new Vec3 { X = 20, Y = 10, Z = 5 },
    HitPlayerID = 5,
    InterpolationTime = 100
}
```

But if `Raycast` and `Vec3` messages had constructors, it was possible to do it like this:
```
var raycast = new Raycast(new Vec3(10, 20, 30), new Vec3(20, 10, 5), 5, 100);
```
Which is much shorter. 

You might think that the current version (without constructors) is better because it is clear and explicit which value is assigned to which field. But in real scenarios, we are not using literals to assign values. So that's not a problem.

Also, this must be an option. No one wants a constructor for a message that contains 10+ fields. 

Best Regards.

