Few ways to do this:

You could fix up the package name to be smaller and use the java package file option to set the namespace that way.

```proto
syntax = "proto3";

import "google/protobuf/any.proto";

package wordlist;

option java_package = "com.list.words.pojos";
```

Maybe even shrink the name of the message names a little by putting protos in another namespace:

```proto
option java_package = "com.list.words.pojos.protos";

message CompositeLine {
  string specifier = 1;
  repeated google.protobuf.Any lines = 2;
}

message Line {
  string specifier = 1;
  repeated string words = 2;
}
```

Or you could make a custom Any type that uses numbers to describe the message type like so:

You'll need an enum value option to hold a type name.
```proto
extend google.protobuf.EnumValueOptions {
    string type_name = 123456; // put whatever number you want here
}
```
Then you need an enum for each set of messages, where each of the values has the type name option set that we defined before.
```proto
enum Pojos {
     UNKNOWN = 0;
     COMPOSITE_LINE = 1 [(type_name) = "com.list.words.pojos.CompositeLineDto"];
     LINE = 2 [(type_name) = "com.list.words.pojos.LineDto"];
}
message AnyPojo {
    Pojos type_num = 1;
    bytes value = 2;
}
```
From there in Java you can make our own pack/unpack method that looks at the value and unpacks accordingly, but done with pseudo-code because I don't know Java (you'll get the idea though).
```cs
partial class AnyPojo {
    public static AnyPojo pack(Message message) {
        ByteString value = message.toByteString();
        String fullName = message.getDescriptorForType().getFullName();
        EnumDescriptor pojoTypeValueDescriptor = Pojos.getDescriptor().getValues().find(v => v.getOptions().getExtension(type_name) == full_name);
        return AnyPojo.newBuilder()
            .setTypeNum(Pojos.valueOf(pojoTypeValueDescriptor))
            .setValue(value)
            .build();
    }
    public bool is<T>() {
        EnumValueDescriptor value = this.getValue().getValueDescriptor();
        return value.getOptions().getExtension(type_name) == T.getDescriptor().getFullName();
    }
    public T unpack<T>(ByteString value) {
        if (this.is<T>()) {
            return T.getParser().parse(value);
        } else {
            // idk return null or throw an exception
        }
    }
}
```

To make pack a bit faster you may also use some sort of caching for each value's string to it's corresponding enum value.

