`AbstractMessageLite implements MessageLite, Externalizable`
or
`MessageLite extends MessageLiteOrBuilder, Externalizable`

Then
```java
abstract class AbstractMessageLite {

  @Override
  public void writeExternal(ObjectOutput out) throws IOException {
    // just call this.writeTo(…);
  }

  @Override
  public void readExternal(ObjectInput in) throws IOException, ClassNotFoundException {
    // just call this.parseFrom(…);
  }

}
```

**Describe alternatives you've considered**

1. Writing mappers for EACH CLASS, which might break.
   Disadvantage: You'd have to keep everything in sync. Very tight coupling.
2. Extending the class with a delegator pattern.
   Disadvantage: But the documentation says to not do this. Also, you'd have to keep the methods in sync. Very tight coupling.
3. Use your own value beans / immutables from your application and make those externalizable.
   Disadvantage: The'd have to know the mapper class  to the suitable Protobuf class. Tight coupling to a specific mapper.

I cannot think of an alternative without tight coupling, wheras a protobuf class implementing Externalizable seems easy to do. Also, this was  also done for C# in https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/issues/5148, see PR https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/pull/5208.

