Version 3.10.1, C++, gcc 9.2 on Debian 9

When compiling the following valid protocol buffer to C++:
```
syntax = "proto3";

message Foo_Bar {
    message Blubb {
    }
}

message Foo {
    message Bar_Blubb {
    }
}
```
the sub messages `Foo_Bar.Blubb` and `Foo.Bar_Blubb` are compiled into two separate classes with the same name `Foo_Bar_Blubb`. This will cause `error: redefinition of ´class Foo_Bar_Blubb´`.

This could be prevented by not using a simple underscore for the nesting but for example

-  using namespaces/nested classes: --> `Foo_Bar::Blubb` and `Foo::Bar_Blubb`,
-  escaping underscores through duplication: --> `Foo__Bar_Blubb` and `Foo_Bar__Blubb` or
-  using enumeration: --> `Foo_Bar_Blubb0` and `Foo_Bar_Blubb1` (though new name collisions might arise).

The first option is probably the cleanest and most C++-like. However, none of them is backwards-compatible.

