Worth noting: inner classes can't have same name as outer class in Java. That is, this is illegal:

```
public class Hello {

  public static class Hello {
  
  }

}
```

I ran into the same problem. There is a gRPC contract that is used by several services in different languages (Go, C#) and there is no way to use this contract by Java applications. At this moment there is only one solution: using your own fork of the original protofile with a different message names.

There is a strange syntax restriction in Java that does not allow to use nested classes with the same name as the outer class.

But there is no restriction at the java bytecode level, because each nested class will be compiled into a separated file with a dollar sign as a class name separator.
For example:

```java
public class TestClass {  
    public static final class MyEmbeddedClass {  
        public static final class ThirdEmbeddedClass {  
        }    
    }  
}
```

Will be compiled into three different `.class` files:

```
TestClass$MyEmbeddedClass$ThirdEmbeddedClass.class  
TestClass$MyEmbeddedClass.class  
TestClass.class
```

You can easily create a class with `$` in the name, because `$` is valid character for a java class name.

Because of that, this piece of Kotlin code works fine.
```kotlin
class Sample {  
    class Sample {  
        class Sample {  
        }    
    }  
}
```

And the output of Kotlin compiler will looks like that:

```
Sample$Sample$Sample.class  
Sample$Sample.class  
Sample.class
```

---

So, based on that, i have only one suggestion how to fix that without breaking compatibility: allow users to provide an additional generator parameter that forces all generated classes to be prefixed with the parent class name. 

Like that:
`protoc -java_out=force_class_name_path:target/ example.proto`

And for the input like:

```protobuf
message Request {
    string message = 1;
    Payload payload = 2;
}

message Payload {
    int64 id = 1;
    repeated Payload innerPayloads = 2;

    message Payload {
        string name = 1;
    }
}
```

Expected result will be like that:

```java
public final class Payload {
    public static final class PayloadPayload {
    }
}
```

    public sealed partial class Test
    {
        public const int ChildrenFieldNumber = 2;
        private static readonly FieldCodec<int> _repeated_children_codec
            = FieldCodec.ForInt32(18);
        private RepeatedField<int> children_ = new RepeatedField<int>();
        [global::System.Diagnostics.DebuggerNonUserCodeAttribute]
        public RepeatedField<int> Children
        {
            get { return children_; }
            set { children_ = value; }
        }
    }

if i put the code above in the partial class everything works flawlessly, what is the problem with readonly and setter.

> We (Google) used to have mutable protos. Experience taught us this was a very painful mistake. Immutability is a deliberate feature.

It is still possible to mutate a `RepeatedField` with `Add` and `AddRange` methods similar to how it is possible to mutate other fields such as primitives or sub-messages. The only thing the current design ensures is that the reference to the list is immutable. Maybe Java uses builders and keeps the proto class immutable but in C# a proto class is definitely not immutable.

```c#
SomeProto proto = new SomeProto();
// Mutate some fields
user.Id = 4;
user.Name = "some name";
```

I am curious why the following doesn't work or what issues it might cause:

```c#
private List<string> _names = new List<string>();
public List<string> Names {
  get => _names;
  set {
    if (value == null) {
      throw new ArgumentNullException();
    }
    _names = value;
  }
}
```

Not having a setter is causing issues with EFCore. We cannot project database results into our proto DTO objects with repeated fields (there is an open [FR](https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues/16867) on the EFCore side btw):

For example, the following doesn't work because `Roles` is `RepeatedField` without a setter:

```c#
_users.Select(u => new UserProto {
  Roles = { new RoleProto { ... } },
});
```

Even though I used `List` in the example, I think `RepeatedField` is fine. It implements `IList` so integrates well with other code. Sorry for the confusion.

However, not having a setter does cause some issues in EFCore projections. I do understand that there might be some internal assumptions that might be broken with a setter so feel free to close this bug. I hope EFCore team supports `MemberMemberBinding` soon: https://github.com/dotnet/efcore/issues/16867. In the meantime, we are projecting first to an anonymous type and then to proto (outside of the LINQ expression) as a workaround:

```c#
var users = dbContext.Users
  .Select(u => new {
    Roles = u.Roles.Select(r => new UserRoleProto {
      // ...
    }).ToList(),
  })
  .AsEnumerable()
  .Select(u => new UserProto {
    Roles = { u.Roles },
  })
  .ToList();
```

You can use the wrapper classes:

```
message Foo
{
    google.protobuf.StringValue bar = 1;
}
```
or you can wrap your scalars in a singleton oneof:

```
message Foo
{
    oneof bar_opt { string bar = 1; }
}
```
Either way, you are looking to see if the field is set or not and then you can interpret that as meaning null in some contexts.  For example, on an update operation you can use a google.protobuf.FieldMask to specify that you are updating Foo.bar, but Foo.bar is absent / unset in the corresponding Foo.  In that situation, you can interpret this to mean "set Foo.bar on this resource to null."

The generated code for some languages (e.g. - Java) is not as friendly with oneofs and their scalar members as others (e.g. - Python).  If you use the [well known wrapper types](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/wrappers.proto), then you will more likely get similar treatment across all languages.

