@jtattermusch This code generation makes less sense for proto2 files -- in particular instead of writing the logical code:

    message.Foo = foo;  // from a method parameter

You have to instead write:

    if (foo == null) message.ClearFoo(); else message.Foo = foo;

Or, if you happen to know that `message` is currently clear, you can conditionally perform the assignment:

    if (foo != null) message.Foo = foo;

But still -- for both proto2 and proto3 -- all of these things mean that you cannot use C# [object initialization syntax](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/classes-and-structs/how-to-initialize-objects-by-using-an-object-initializer) -- because there is no way to indicate conditional setting of a property that way.

    return new MyProtoMessage()
    {
        Foo = foo,  // oops, can't do this if it might be null
        // now imagine this with >10 properties that might be individually null
    };

This makes protobuf generated classes counter-intuitive and contrary to best practices in C#.

The null check should just be removed.  (Ideally by default, but if necessary, with an option.)  It makes sense that setting null (like with message properties) would result in the property being cleared and not sent in the resulting proto data.  This should not be an error.

