Hi, I'm experiencing a strange issue with `libprotobuf` 3.13.0 built statically for Windows 10 / VS 2019. 

The relevant parts of our (admittedly suboptimal) module structure are as follows:

1. CMake shared library target, say target A, that builds generated c++ protobuf files, and links against `protobuf::libprotobuf` statically via CMake.
2. CMake application target, say target B, that links target A dynamically, and also links `protobuf::libprotobuf` statically (as generated C++ protobuf headers contain includes pointing to `libprotobuf` headers).

All targets build with C++17.

When building said module structure, we are able to build target A just fine, but when it comes to building target B, we see the following error during compile (full include paths hidden)

```
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(84,19): error C4430: missing type specifier - int assumed. Note: C++ does not support default-int
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(84,19): error C2059: syntax error: 'return'
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(85,22): error C2143: syntax error: missing ';' before '{'
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(85,22): error C2447: '{': missing function header (old-style formal list?)
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(96,20): error C4430: missing type specifier - int assumed. Note: C++ does not support default-int
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(96,20): error C2059: syntax error: 'return'
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(97,39): error C2143: syntax error: missing ';' before '{'
...\include\google\protobuf\stubs\callback.h(97,39): error C2447: '{': missing function header (old-style formal list?)
```

Looking at `callback.h`, I think the deleted constructors coming from the macro `GOOGLE_DISALLOW_EVIL_CONSTRUCTORS` are causing issues:

```c++
class PROTOBUF_EXPORT Closure {
 public:
  Closure() {}
  virtual ~Closure();

  virtual void Run() = 0;

 private:
  GOOGLE_DISALLOW_EVIL_CONSTRUCTORS(Closure);
};

// the below is line 84 - the first error referenced above - making me think GOOGLE_DISALLOW_EVIL_CONSTRUCTORS() is not expanding
template<typename R>
class ResultCallback {
 public:
  ResultCallback() {}
  virtual ~ResultCallback() {}

  virtual R Run() = 0;

 private:
  GOOGLE_DISALLOW_EVIL_CONSTRUCTORS(ResultCallback);
};
```

My guess is that during the compile of target B, the macro is not being expanded properly. While the best solution here might be to rework the module structure mentioned above, I'm wondering if you might have any ideas as to how to mitigate this issue without such a refactor. Thank you in advance.

**What version of protobuf and what language are you using?**
Version: v3.13.0
Language: C#

**What operating system (Linux, Windows, ...) and version?**
Reproducible on any version

**What runtime / compiler are you using (e.g., python version or gcc version)**
.NET Core

**What did you do?**
There is a problem with the deserialization of Int32 field when it contains a negative value. This happens only on very specific circumstances - when the field is serialized across the buffer (span) boundaries (when it starts at the and of one span and continues at the beginning of the next span). Deserialization thinks that the whole field fits to the first span, but this is not true and after deserialization, the buffer pointer is beyond the buffer size. 

**What did you expect to see**
Successful deserialization of the message on the client

**What did you see instead?**
Exception:
System.IndexOutOfRangeException: Index was outside the bounds of the array.
   at Google.Protobuf.ParsingPrimitives.ReadRawByte(ReadOnlySpan`1& buffer, ParserInternalState& state)
   at Google.Protobuf.ParsingPrimitives.ParseRawVarint32SlowPath(ReadOnlySpan`1& buffer, ParserInternalState& state)
   at Google.Protobuf.ParsingPrimitivesWrappers.ReadUInt32WrapperSlow(ReadOnlySpan`1& buffer, ParserInternalState& state)
   at Google.Protobuf.ParsingPrimitivesWrappers.ReadInt32Wrapper(ReadOnlySpan`1& buffer, ParserInternalState& state)

**Details**
This is the code for int32 serialization. Negative values are serialized as Varint64, it can be 11 bytes long.

> public static void WriteInt32(ref Span<byte> buffer, ref WriterInternalState state, int value)
        {
            if (value >= 0)
            {
                WriteRawVarint32(ref buffer, ref state, (uint)value);
            }
            else
            {
                // Must sign-extend.
                WriteRawVarint64(ref buffer, ref state, (ulong)value);
            }
        }

But deserialization do not count with this. ReadUInt32Wrapper function has this condition there:

> // length:1 + tag:1 + value:5(varint32-max) = 7 bytes
            if (state.bufferPos + 7 <= state.bufferSize)
            {
// The entire wrapper message is already contained in buffer.

... which is not true for negative values.

The key in `MapPair` should [not be const](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/7b352d318b9ae8f65ddc85b7eb7e9921911cec6f/src/google/protobuf/map.h#L352). It makes for example using `std::transform` etc. impossible.

```

  google::protobuf::MapPair<int, int> mm(1,2);
  google::protobuf::MapPair<int, int> nn(1,2);

  nn = mm; // Does not compile!
  
  google::protobuf::Map<int, int> map;
  map.insert(1,1);
  
  *map.begin() = nn; // Does not compile!
```

