**Specification**

I assume that one of the reasons this wasn't implemented is because the [specification ](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/encoding#packed) states that repeated fields do not have to be serialized as a contiguous block

> If a proto2 message definition has repeated elements (without the [packed=true] option), the encoded message has zero or more key-value pairs with the same field number. These repeated values do not have to appear consecutively; they may be interleaved with other fields. The order of the elements with respect to each other is preserved when parsing, though the ordering with respect to other fields is lost. In proto3, repeated fields use packed encoding, which you can read about below.

This means that if there is a repeated field `repeated Type list = 1`, messages serialized as `1,1,1,2,3` are equivalent to `1,2,1,3,1`. Implementing a merge-replace would cause issues with the second case. I don't think that there are any current implementations that serialize messages like this, but there are probably many users that rely on this mechanism for appending data to existing messages.

The `packed=true` tag results in a more efficient encoding `[key][length][<contiguous-list-of-all-values>]` for scalar types, but my understanding is that packed fields can also be split into multiple packed blocks.

**Brainstorming**

It could be interesting to add an option for the merge behavior, e.g., `repeated Type field = 1 [on_merge = APPEND || REPLACE];` where REPLACE would clear and replace the list with new content, i.e., similar behavior to merging scalars.

Protoc 3.17.3 generates the following parsing code for `repeated NestedMessage repeated_nested_message  = 48;`

```java
// parsing code generated for a repeated message
case 386: {
  if (!((mutable_bitField1_ & 0x00000100) != 0)) {
    repeatedNestedMessage_ = new java.util.ArrayList<protos.test.protobuf.TestAllTypes.NestedMessage>();
    mutable_bitField1_ |= 0x00000100;
  }
  repeatedNestedMessage_.add(
      input.readMessage(protos.test.protobuf.TestAllTypes.NestedMessage.PARSER, extensionRegistry));
  break;
}
```

It looks like the switch statement is executed every time a tag is encountered, so you'd have to add something to keep track of whether you are inside a continuous block of repeated values. The easiest way I can think of would be to check whether the tag has changed since the last iteration, e.g., something like

```java
// untested example
boolean done = false;
int previousTag = 0;
while (!done) {
  int tag = input.readTag();
  switch (tag) {
    // ... other cases
    case 386: {
    if (!((mutable_bitField1_ & 0x00000100) != 0)) {
      repeatedNestedMessage_ = new java.util.ArrayList<protos.test.protobuf.TestAllTypes.NestedMessage>();
      mutable_bitField1_ |= 0x00000100;
    }
    if (previousTag != tag) { // generate if on_merge == REPLACE
      repeatedNestedMessage_.clear();
    }
    repeatedNestedMessage_.add(
        input.readMessage(protos.test.protobuf.TestAllTypes.NestedMessage.PARSER, extensionRegistry));
    break;
  }
  // ... other cases
  }
  previousTag = tag;
}
```

It has been a while since I looked at the protoc sources, but as far as I remember you would need to take a look at the case statement generation in [ImmutableMessageGenerator::GenerateParsingConstructor](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/compiler/java/java_message.cc#L1229-L1235) and the `Repeated<xxx>Field::GenerateParsingCode` implementations, e.g., [RepeatedImmutableMessageFieldGenerator::GenerateParsingCode](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/compiler/java/java_message_field.cc#L1326-L1345) for repeated message fields (this part is a bit of a mess for code duplication).

edit: of course the `mergeFrom` method would also have to be changed to match parsing behavior

This worked for my team:

protobuf {

    protoc {
        artifact = if (project.hasProperty("protocPlatform")) {
            "com.google.protobuf:protoc:${protobuf_version}:osx-x86_64"
        } else {
            "com.google.protobuf:protoc:${protobuf_version}"
        }
    }

    generateProtoTasks {
        all().forEach { task ->

            task.builtins {
                id("java") {
                    option("lite")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// It checks if your pc is mac silicon or not and adds the correct configuration.

