@jtattermusch  

When the field is defined as sint32, some values will overflow after zigzag encoding (the value will become negative at this time), for example, Integer.MAX_VALUE / 2 + 1, then the writeUInt32NoTag method will be called to complete the writing（@see https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/java/core/src/main/java/com/google/protobuf/CodedOutputStream.java#L399-L401）：

```
  /** Write a {@code sint32} field to the stream. */
  public final void writeSInt32NoTag(final int value) throws IOException {
    writeUInt32NoTag(encodeZigZag32(value));
  }
```

So my question is, why the treatment of negative numbers is different when the type is int32 and sint32. If the type is int32, negative numbers will be written to 10 bytes; but when the type is sint32, negative numbers are only written 5 bytes。

