Yes, we've generated partial classes forever as far as I remember (even in my previous proto2 implementation).

I very specifically called out the case where one message contains a field of another message type, because that's where the caller *doesn't* specify the type. For example:

```proto
message Outer {
    Inner inner = 1;
}

message Inner {
    int32 x = 1;
}
```

```csharp
var outer = new OuterDerived
{
    Inner = new InnerDerived { X = 1 }
};
```

Protobuf has no notion of inheritance, so while it could (potentially) be fine to serialize and deserialize as:

```csharp
var bytes = outer.ToByteArray();
OuterDerived parsed = OuterDerived.Parser.ParseFrom(bytes);
```

... what would the execution-time type of `parsed.Inner` be? There's no standard way to serialize the additional information that you'd want it to be deserialized as an `InnerDerived`.

This is the sort of complexity that is completely side-stepped by sealing the classes - there's just no question of which type to deserialize as.

One other thing on the Enum issue specifically. Because protobuf schemas can diverge between sender and receiver, enum values can appear that diverge from your enum definition.

In proto3, [enum fields are truly open](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/reference/python-generated#enum): they can contain any `int32` value. So there needs to be a way of representing a proto enum value as a bare integer, even if it is outside the domain of what was defined in the `.proto` file.

For example, protobuf enum fields (at least in proto3) will accept bare integers that do not correspond to any enumerator in the .proto file:

```protobuf
// test.proto
syntax = "proto3";

enum E {
  UNSPECIFIED = 0;
  ONE = 1;
  TWO = 2;
}

message M {
  E e = 1;
}
```

```python
# test.py
import test_pb2

m = test_pb2.m()

# Setter can accept a bare int outside the domain:
m.e = 12345

# Getter can return a bare int outside the domain:
x = m.e
```

Can the enum typing solution in protobuf-mypy accomodate this?

proto2 enums are closed (unexpected enums at parse time are put into the unknown field set instead of the field), so a typing solution that excludes bare integers would be more appropriate there.

