Hi @hellt I found this: [Google API Improvement Proposals › General AIPs › Enumerations](https://google.aip.dev/126#:~:text=Enums%20which%20will%20only%20be,code%2C%20which%20is%20unnecessarily%20verbose.).

The generated `dayofweek.pb.h` looks like this:

```cpp
namespace google {
namespace type {
enum DayOfWeek : int {
  DAY_OF_WEEK_UNSPECIFIED = 0,
  MONDAY = 1,
  TUESDAY = 2,
  WEDNESDAY = 3,
  THURSDAY = 4,
  FRIDAY = 5,
  SATURDAY = 6,
  SUNDAY = 7,
  DayOfWeek_INT_MIN_SENTINEL_DO_NOT_USE_ = std::numeric_limits<::int32_t>::min(),
  DayOfWeek_INT_MAX_SENTINEL_DO_NOT_USE_ = std::numeric_limits<::int32_t>::max(),
};
} // namespace type
} // namespace google
```

The definition of `DayOfWeek` is wrapped in namespace `google::type`, so technically as long as you can make sure that there is no conflict among the enum values in the same protobuf package (C++ namespace), you can omit the prefix. But according to the protobuf style guide and the AIP, we should still prefix every enum value for package-level enums. That’s also what I don’t understand.

IIUC, in all runtimes that support proto2, all fields in a proto2 message provide explicit presence information (unlike proto3). Furthermore, all extendable messages are proto2 messages (while you can define extensions in proto3, the extended message can only be defined in proto2). So it follows that all extensions behave the desired way and that the `proto3_optional` element on the field descriptor should always be false.

@haberman, I think I could put up a PR to fix this. But before doing so, I wonder what you think the fix looks like. My personal opinion is that the `optional` keyword should simply be disallowed for extensions in proto3 files, since it could be confusing that fields labeled thusly are different than normal fields with that label (since normal fields will be `proto3_optional`).

But an alternate "fix" would be to allow the keyword but effectively ignore it -- or rather treat is as if in a proto2 source file. So it's a normal optional field (not `proto3_optional`), just as if the label were absent (with maybe the only observable difference being that source code info could include a source location for the label).

