Our JSON format uses base64 already, so there is some precedent there. Such a change would require coordination across languages, since several languages implement text format parsers.

Unfortunately the syntax you propose would be non-trivial to implement. The text format parser in C++ [shares a lexer with `.proto` files](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/master/src/google/protobuf/io/tokenizer.h). This shared tokenizer doesn't accept unquoted bare strings like this. It would almost parse as an identifier, but `+` and `/` would be problematic, as would a base64 string starting with a digit.

More technically feasible within this framework could be something like:

```
session {
  session_hash: b64 "ToHI5cyx9CHISYDmyf6ufPCh9ag4XjFgmKq1zO1ObW4"
  nonce: b64 "gxJAwsK083yfSAVZN7s95w"
  user_session {
  # ....
    id: b64 "Vl5d8p/oN3I+NRa3vGVhrA"
  }
}
```

