Your second concern is noted, but I figure this would be used for more advanced users wanting to optimize further, so we can let the user manage the memory themselves in those cases. We already have the `release_foo` API's available, so I think this is similar, letting user own the memory themselves. True, you couldn't use `RepeatedField.set_allocated` with `std::vector`, but you could with `ConstRepeatedField`, with own=false. Non-STL structures may allow safe borrowing, which is why I suggested the `RepeatedField.set_allocated` interface (e.g. if user manually created an array). Some examples:

