These types actually already have support for nullability. In proto3, any type that is a message has built in support for nullability. Because Timestamp and Duration are messages, it works out of the box.

Example: https://github.com/mattwelke/dart-proto-example

Here's my test message, based on the official Dart getting started guide:

```proto3
syntax = "proto3";
package tutorial;

import "google/protobuf/timestamp.proto";
import "google/protobuf/wrappers.proto";

message Person {
  string                      first_name      = 1;
  google.protobuf.StringValue last_name       = 2;
  google.protobuf.Timestamp   graduation_date = 3;
}
```

I use a `string` and a `StringValue` to demonstrate the difference between a `string`, which is a primitive and does not support nullability, and a `StringValue` which because it's a message (a wrapper message) does support it.

In the `main.dart` file:

```dart
import 'google/protobuf/timestamp.pb.dart';
import 'google/protobuf/wrappers.pb.dart';
import 'person.pb.dart';

void main() {
  final john = new Person(firstName: "John");
  print("John has last name: ${john.hasLastName()}");
  print("John is graduate: ${john.hasGraduationDate()}\n");

  final jane =
      new Person(firstName: "Jane", lastName: new StringValue(value: "Doe"));
  print("Jane has last name: ${jane.hasLastName()} (${jane.lastName.value})");
  print("Jane is graduate: ${jane.hasGraduationDate()}\n");

  final jim = new Person(
      firstName: "Jim",
      lastName: new StringValue(value: "Doe"),
      graduationDate: new Timestamp());
  print("Jim has last name: ${jim.hasLastName()} (${jim.lastName.value})");
  print(
      "Jim has graduated: ${jim.hasGraduationDate()} (${jim.graduationDate.seconds})\n");
}
```

When you run it with `dart run lib/src/main.dart`, this is the output:

```
John has last name: false
John is graduate: false

Jane has last name: true (Doe)
Jane is graduate: false

Jim has last name: true (Doe)
Jim has graduated: true (0)
```

Note that despite the lack of a "TimestampValue" wrapper type existing, you still get the `hasGraduationDate` instance method generated. All message fields will get either these "has" functions generated (Java is another example I can think of) or you can compare the field with the language's "null" value (Go and C# come to mind). It's up the implementer of the language's Protobuf plugin. In this case, the implementers of the Dart plugin went with the "has method" approach.

This is why Google only created wrapper messages for `double`, `float`, `int64`, `uint64`, `int32`, `uint32`, `bool`, `string`, and `bytes`. They're Protobuf primitives, so it's impossible for any implementation to generate either the "has methods" or the comparisons to the null value. So Google stepped in to make the wrapper types so that people wouldn't have to. This is nice because now a StringValue in one project is the same type as a StringValue in another project, even if they're from different parties. That saves the parties from having to write glue code if we wanted to assign the value of one field in one message to another field in another message.

A bit off topic, but note that since release 3.15, the "optional" keyword came back, and you can use that for nullable primitives without having to use the wrapper type, as long as the plugin you're using also has support for it in proto3. See the accepted answer at https://stackoverflow.com/questions/42622015/how-to-define-an-optional-field-in-protobuf-3?rq=1.

