@elharo - I believe it needs to be backported so that 3.18.0 stops (incorrectly) making the implicit claim "I am the latest version of this library supporting any version of Python" by not including `python_requires`.

That is: until 3.18.0 has an explicit value for `python_requires` - it will continue to be installed for anyone on 2.7 that isn't version locking / constraining it to `<3.18.0`.

Otherwise, when pip looks at the latest version you're talking about where it is "fixed" - it will say "oops, that one has an incompatible `python_requires`, how about the one before it - 3.18.0? Yep - no `python_requires` - looks good, let's install that one". Oops... :)

There is similar problem with using restricted C++ keywords in message names, and then using parameter name that conflicts with class name after keyword-related collision mitigation is applied.

Example:
```
message sizeof {
    required bool sizeof_ = 1;
}
```

`protoc` detects that `sizeof` is restricted keyword in C++ and applies mitigation by appending suffix `_`, but it may conflict with parameter of such name, causing collision between class default constructor and parameters getter.
Admittedly this is rather extreme edge case.

