uses underlying mergeTimestamp method that only supports parsing data in  this format "1970-01-01T00:00:10.000000100Z"

Timestamp value = Timestamps.parse(json.getAsString());
**Solution--**
So to resolve this I have added the ability in this mergeTimestamp method code to check the instance of JsonElement  is JsonObject which  will fetch the value of nanos and second and will create the Time stamp value 
Timestamp.newBuilder().setSeconds(seconds).setNanos(nanos).build();
Else it will do normal parsing as existing code does
Timestamps.parse(json.getAsString());


So its just a enhancing the mergetimestamp method  ability to handle both type of data jsonobject in form of nanos and seconds and as well as normal object of String.


And now after adding this we are able to use the mergeTimestamp of protobuf util for conversion from Avro to protobytes.
Code changes and testcase classes have been updated in below PR
https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/pull/9113

The sample use case is to Illustrate the issue  when we are creating the time stamp object using Avro GenericRecord and the result of the record when sent in JsonFormat.parser().merge(json, builder)
–

```
public static void main(String... args) throws IOException, NoSuchFieldException, IllegalAccessException {


    Timestamp timestamp = Timestamp.newBuilder().setSeconds(10).setNanos(100).build();

    GenericRecord record = genericRecord(timestamp);


    System.out.println(JsonFormat.printer().print(timestamp));
    System.out.println(record.toString());//This prints the record format //we receive of timestamp which we need to parse that is {"seconds": 10, "nanos": 100}

    Timestamp.Builder builder = Timestamp.newBuilder();
    JsonFormat.parser().merge(JsonFormat.printer().print(timestamp), builder);
    System.out.println(builder.build());//this prints the record after parsing in new code seconds: 10 nanos: 100 it is as expected.


    Timestamp.Builder builder2 = Timestamp.newBuilder();
    JsonFormat.parser().merge(JsonFormat.printer().print(timestamp), builder);

    System.out.println(builder2.build());

}
public static GenericRecord genericRecord(Message message) throws IOException {
    Class<Message> tClass = (Class<Message>) message.getClass();
    ProtobufDatumWriter<Message> datumWriter = new ProtobufDatumWriter<>(tClass);
    GenericDatumReader<GenericRecord> genericDatumReader = new GenericDatumReader<>(new ProtobufDatumReader<>(tClass).getSchema());
    return getGenericRecord(message, datumWriter, genericDatumReader);
}

private static <T extends Message> GenericRecord getGenericRecord(T message, ProtobufDatumWriter<T> writer, GenericDatumReader<GenericRecord> reader) throws IOException {
    ByteArrayOutputStream os = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
    Encoder e = EncoderFactory.get().binaryEncoder(os, null);
    writer.write(message, e);
    e.flush();
    return reader.read(null, DecoderFactory.get().binaryDecoder(new ByteArrayInputStream(os.toByteArray()), null));
}

