I was reading the [Encoding specs](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/encoding#structure) and noticed that protobuf-messages are not self-delimiting (the parser has no way of knowing where the message ends and will simply read until EOF/EOS is encountered). Furthermore, a protobuf message seems to essentially only be a sequence of key-value pairs where each key-value pair is self-delimiting.

This got me wondering whether it was possible to comply concatenate Protobuf messages in a single stream in a way that the decoding side would treat the entire stream as a single message.

Consider for instance this message definition:
```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

message MainMessage {
	int32 myNumber = 1;
    string myString = 2;
	bytes myBlob = 3;
	uint32 myOtherNumber = 4;
}

message Part {
	bytes myBlob = 3;
	uint32 myOtherNumber = 4;
}
```

Now create a message object of type `MainMessage` and set it's `myNumber` and `myString` fields only. Also create a `Part` message and set its `myBlob` and `myOtherNumber` fields.
Could we now serialize the `MainMessage` object and append to the serialized output the serialized version of the `Part` message such that a deserialization as `MainMessage` would have all four fields populated (as previously set on the two separate message instances)?

