@willfaught in my case, I am using this to essentially re-serialize messages only partly. So I have a client that expects a full message but the server sends that same message to multiple clients. However, for each client some of the fields of said message will vary ever so slightly. If the server was just using a single protobuf message object and change the required fields using the standard methods, it would have to re-encode the entire message into wire-format every time (involving copying of non-trivially sized byte strings).
Using the trick above, I can make sure that the parts of the message that are subject to change are at the end of the message and then I only have to remember where the constant part of the message ends and just overwrite the rest with a re-serialized partial message that only contains the changed fields.

This could also have been solved by splitting the message in the first place, but from a client's point of view, it is semantically much more logical to have all that information grouped together as a single message.

@Krzmbrzl The protobuf doc [says](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/encoding#optional) it's sometimes useful to concatenate wire format data when you don't have the schema:

>The effect of these rules is that parsing the concatenation of two encoded messages produces exactly the same result as if you had parsed the two messages separately and merged the resulting objects. [...] This property is occasionally useful, as it allows you to merge two messages even if you do not know their types.

 Have you ever found or observed such a case/need?

