      return b < (byte) 0xE0;
    }

    /**
     * Returns whether this is a three-byte codepoint with the form '110XXXXX' iff {@link
     * #isTwoBytes(byte)} is false.
     */
    private static boolean isThreeBytes(byte b) {
      return b < (byte) 0xF0;
    }
```

I suspect (need to verify) that these private methods are only used in order (i.e. first test one byte, then two bytes, then three bytes) and that makes this logic OK for the actual uses.

Oh, how could I forget, `Optional`s!

`set` doesn't accept `null`, which makes sense. But it also means that we have to do:

```java
Optional<Baz> getBaz(){...};

Foo.Builder fooBuilder = Foo.newBuilder();
getBaz().ifPresent(fooBuilder::setBaz);
return fooBuilder.build();
```

whereas it would be much nicer to be able to write:

```java
return Foo.newBuilder().setBaz(getBaz()).build();
```

