> (Returning object would allow the caller to get a bit further, for sure - but then if they tried to cast it to their expected type, it would still have a problem. And I'd be worried about trying too hard to assume that two proto-based types were actually identical, based on the FQN - they could easily be different versions of the same original proto.)

I think automatically changing type is probably too magical. But I think a non-generic method that returns the value as object gives the caller flexibility. If that was supported then I'd have consumer code that is something like:

```cs
const int HttpRuleFieldId = 72295728;

object extension = descriptor.GetOptions().NonGenericGetMethod(HttpRuleFieldId);
if (extension is HttpRule r)
{
    // typed value matches expected type
    return r;
}
else if (extension is IMessage m)
{
    // typed value is different. convert to bytes and parse using expected type
    byte[] payload = m.ToByteArray();
    return HttpRule.Descriptor.Parse(payload);
}
else
{
    return null;
}
```

Edit: I took a brief look at the extension code and it looks like all values implement `IExtensionValue` which has a `WriteTo` method.

An alternative idea is an API that returns extensions as their underlying bytes.

Okay, I've implemented this now as a proof of concept (#9635), and it works fine. The `HttpRuleChecker` code becomes:

```csharp
public static class HttpRuleChecker
{
    public static HttpRule? GetHttpRule(MethodDescriptor methodDescriptor)
    {
        var options = methodDescriptor.GetOptions();
        object? extension = options?.GetExtensionValue(AnnotationsExtensions.Http.FieldNumber);
        return extension switch
        {
            HttpRule rule => rule,
            IMessage message => HttpRule.Parser.ParseFrom(message.ToByteArray()),
            _ => null
        };
    }

    public static bool HasHttpRule(MethodDescriptor methodDescriptor) =>
        GetHttpRule(methodDescriptor) is not null;
}
```

The code is simple, it works, and it definitely addresses this specific use case. My only question is how frequently this use case will actually occur, or whether it is useful in any other situations. I suspect that any time there are multiple generated versions of the same proto, you could easily run into other problems anyway - so if those are avoidable (in this case e.g. by changing the documentation) then I think that would be preferable.

In terms of other situations, I can imagine non-generic access being useful if we exposed the extension registry more widely. For example, you could then say:

```csharp
foreach (var extension in MethodOptions.Parser.Extensions)
{
    Console.WriteLine(options.GetExtensionValue(extension.FieldNumber));
}
```

Is that a sufficiently-compelling use case to drive this? I'm not sure. I'm happy to be convinced that the use case that prompted this is actually going to be wide-spread in "otherwise correct" scenarios, but I'm not sure about making the change if we really don't want users to ever get into the situation of needing this anyway.

