I don't understand the explanation. What's stopping you from replacing the generated Kotlin implementation from, say
```kotlin
    /**
     * `optional int32 quantity = 8;`
     */
    public var quantity: kotlin.Int
      @JvmName("getQuantity")
      get() = _builder.getQuantity()
      @JvmName("setQuantity")
      set(value) {
        _builder.setQuantity(value)
      }
```

to

```kotlin
    /**
     * `optional int32 quantity = 8;`
     */
    public var quantity: kotlin.Int?
      @JvmName("getQuantity")
      get() = if (_builder.hasQuantity()) _builder.getQuantity() else null
      @JvmName("setQuantity")
      set(value) {
        if (value == null) _builder.clearQuantity() else _builder.setQuantity(value)
      }
```

This is a major pain for Kotlin developers used to proper null-safety who have to remember to call `hasQuantity()` to check for what appears to be a not-nullable property `quantity`

Keep in mind that [primitive fields can have default values](https://protobuf.dev/programming-guides/proto2/#optional) (not in proto3, but in proto2 and the upcoming [editions](https://protobuf.dev/news/2023-06-29/) version of proto):

```proto
message Foo {
    optional int32 quantity = 8 [default = "123"];
}
```

If we make the `int32` getter return `null` for unset values, then we have no way of returning the default when it is appropriate to do so.

Optional is not the same as nullable, because primitive fields have default values which must be returned in the case of an unset field.

I think the problem here is with `End()`, as it points past-the-end and not to a real `U`.
`Begin()` should always return a pointer to a valid object.

I think a solution is to calculate end via addition from a valid pointer instead.
Eg

```
   template <typename U>
   U* End() const {
-    int begin = BeginOffset<U>(), end = EndOffset<U>();
-    if (begin == end) return nullptr;
-    return reinterpret_cast<U*>(data() + end);
+    return Begin<U>() + (EndOffset<U>() - BeginOffset<U>()) / sizeof(U);
   }
```

It might be faster if someone that can easily reproduce this issue can test the patch.

