```

The code that generates synthetic oneofs [only looks at the names of other fields and oneofs](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/aa22c5e0de189b74271335eea179e5321d88768e/src/google/protobuf/compiler/parser.cc#L778-L784). So the following file successfully compiles:
```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

message Foo {
  optional string oo = 1;

  oneof _oo {
    string X_oo = 2;
  }
}
```
(The synthetic oneof ends up with a name of `XX_oo` to avoid conflicting with the oneof and field already declared.)

However, none of the following files will compile. They all fail with a very mysterious error message that `_oo` is already defined (mysterious unless you are familiar with the implementation details of how proto3 optional fields are represented in the descriptor).
```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

message Foo {
  optional string oo = 1;

  message _oo {}
}
```

```protobuf
syntax = "proto3";

message Foo {
  optional string oo = 1;

  enum _oo {Z = 0;}
}
```

