Although the `default` field pseudo-option _looks like_ an option, it is handled differently. In particular, it allows a larger range of values for integer literals when the field's type is a float or double.

Take this example file:
```protobuf
syntax = "proto2";

message Foo {
  optional double a = 1 [default=18446744073709551615];
  optional double b = 2 [default=18446744073709551616];
  optional double c = 3 [default=-18446744073709551615];
  optional double d = 4 [default=-18446744073709551616];

  optional string w = 5 [(d)=18446744073709551615];
  optional string x = 6 [(d)=18446744073709551616];
  optional string y = 7 [(d)=-9223372036854775808];
  optional string z = 8 [(d)=-9223372036854775809];
}

extend google.protobuf.FieldOptions {
  optional double d = 10001;
}
```

Attempting to compile this (`protoc test.proto -o /dev/null`) results in the following errors:
```
test.proto:5:34: Integer out of range.
test.proto:7:35: Integer out of range.
test.proto:10:30: Integer out of range.
test.proto:12:31: Integer out of range.
```

As can be seen, large integer literal values for normal and custom options are required to fit in the range [-2^63, 2^64). But for the `default` field option, the value is allowed to be (-2^64, 2^64). Values less than -2^63 are reported as erroneous only if the field's actual type is an integer. If the field value is a float or double (like in the example) then the overly large value is accepted.

This is due to the order of operations. [Processing a default value](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/main/src/google/protobuf/compiler/parser.cc#L1234) is a different function from [processing an option value into an uninterpreted option](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/main/src/google/protobuf/compiler/parser.cc#L1461). Processing a default value, when the field is a double or float, effectively applies the sign _last_, which is why it allows parsing such a large value (the maximum unsigned 64-bit int). But for normal and custom options, the field type is not yet known, and the representation in `UninterpretedOption` does not allow storing a negative integer that large (the field is an `int64`). So it applies the sign first, which impacts the maximum allowed range when parsing the integer value.

I think there are two options for how to address this:
1. _Always_ limit the range to the lower-bound of signed 64-bit int and the upper-bound of unsigned 64-bit int. In this case, the above line with a value of -18,446,744,073,709,551,615 would be disallowed. This is a potentially breaking change, however, since it means that existing sources that use integer literals in the range (-2^64, -2^63) for float and double default values would suddenly generate compilation errors.
2. _Always_ allow overly large decimal literals and assume they are doubles if they are out of range. This is more like most programming languages, where the error would happen when trying to assign the excessively large value to a type that cannot accept it (e.g. assigning it to a int field). Unfortunately, this results in a confusing error message for the normal/custom option case, because we have to put the value into the `UninterpretedOption` intermediate representation. This means that we _lose_ the knowledge that it was originally an integer literal (e.g. no floating point, no exponent) and end up with an error message about the field's type requiring an int value, not a float value. We could probably tweak the wording of the error so it is less confusing in the case of an excessively large int literal.

I'm happy to open a PR with either of these choices. In fact, I already implemented option 2 (and realized that the error message, without further tweaks, is a bit confusing). And option 1 is even easier to implement, but has the backwards compatibility issue.

