>  If a caller adds items from e.g. `Dictionary<T>.Keys`, which is of `ICollection<T>`, items are added one by one causing incremental reallocation of its internal array.

It shouldn't - because while `Keys` has a *compile-time* type of `ICollection<T>`, the actual type (`Dictionary<TKey, TValue>.KeyCollection`) *does* implement `ICollection`. Sample test code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Generic;

var dict = new Dictionary<string, string> { { "a", "b" }};

Console.WriteLine(dict.Keys.GetType());   // System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary`2+KeyCollection[System.String,System.String]
Console.WriteLine(dict.Keys is ICollection); // True
```

There's a comment within the code to explain that:

```csharp
// We *could* check for ICollection<T> as well, but very very few collections implement
// ICollection<T> but not ICollection. (HashSet<T> does, for one...)
```

So while it's definitely feasible, I believe it's an optimization which would *relatively* rarely have an impact. I'm happy to look at it at some point, but I consider it relatively low priority. (I'm effectively at the level of a community contributor; this isn't my main role - so I'm afraid there's quite a long wait time for this sort of thing.)

I too encountered the same issue. 

And I noticed discrepancies between debug and release binary builds: debug versions always serialize bools as 0x00 or 0x01,  while release versions will serialize bool to the value of the bool-byte, for example 0x80/0xc0/0xf2/0xfe..

This is likely because protobuf's protocol for bools is same to varints, where a leading 1 in a varint indicates more data. Serializing a bool as 0x80 thus leads to deserialization failure.

## In the `release` mode. I test some implementations of serialization. and all bool-byte is equal to 0x80(128)

### Current implementation, pb version 3.9.x
```
//wire_format_lite.h
 inline uint8* WireFormatLite::WriteBoolNoTagToArray(bool value, uint8* target) {
  return io::CodedOutputStream::WriteVarint32ToArray(value ? 1 : 0, target);
}

//io/coded_stream.h
inline uint8* CodedOutputStream::WriteVarint32ToArray(uint32 value,
                                                        uint8* target) {
  while (value >= 0x80) {
    *target = static_cast<uint8>(value | 0x80);
    value >>= 7;
    ++target;
  }
  *target = static_cast<uint8>(value);
  return target + 1; 
