nodes.body:
This is an expected result of the way that the PHP extension manages memory.

In this code example we have a long-lived message object `$test->_memory_leak_test_msg`. The code repeatedly creates and destroys the `numbers` field. The PHP extension uses arenas to allocate the memory instead of GCing every object individually, so every `RepeatedField` object ends up in a single arena, the same arena used by the message. The arena is not freed until the message is freed.

The best workaround is to periodically clone the message with parse and serialize:

```php
public function ResetProto(){
        $new_Msg = new MemoryLeakTestMsg();
        $new_msg->mergeFromString($this->_memory_leak_test_msg->serializeToString());
	$this->_memory_leak_test_msg = $new_msg;
}
```

The new message will not share an arena with the old message, so the old message's data can be freed immediately.

This issue and workarounds need to be documented better: https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/issues/10359

