@jskeet thank you for your time. As discussed, I created conformance tests: https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/pull/12551

In the process of doing so I observed that map keys containing a `.` character are a problem for binary serialization as well. A reader can't unambiguously read `"map_field.anton.grbin"`. It feels like FieldMask without backticks is not ready to support map keys.

I found a PR that introduced the AIP: https://github.com/aip-dev/google.aip.dev/pull/701 and you were part of the review, so I am confident that I am in good hands here to get the learnings here :)

Extending the example from here: https://google.aip.dev/161#map-fields

```protobuf
message Book {
  // Metadata about the review.
  message Review {
    string bar = 1;
    string text = 2;
  }

  // A map of string review_id to Review object.
  map<string, Review> reviews = 2;
}
```

Let's imagine two hypothetical requests:
1) Insert a new review entry under key `foo.bar`
2) Update the existing review entry under the key `foo` to change the field `bar` to something

I believe that FieldMasks in both 1 and 2 will be equal. In text proto: `paths: "reviews.foo.bar"`.

The problem is that `google.protobuf.FieldMask` uses `repeated string paths = 1;` where each path is a further concatenation of elements with a delimiter `.`.

With the proposed quoting, the two FieldMask will become distinct. In text proto:
```
// request 1
{ paths: "reviews.`foo.bar`" }

// request 2
{ paths: "reviews.`foo`.bar" }
```

Just to add to the record, C# throws an exception too:

```csharp
