Protobuf is heavily used in our business and one of the most important message **Log** is recording all the online events and parameters for a single request in the system(composed of hundreds of micro services). Then **Log** messages are sent into message queue and consumed by lots of downstream application(backend services/big data applications) for monitoring/reporting/training.

After several years of development, there are large numbers of fields added into **Log** and **Log** become a very complicated message with some nested levels of sub-messages. The main pain point currently is that we don't know which fields are actually used by downstream application after compiled into java sources. The field usage is critical for us to do some field management, such as a field should be totally offlined if it was still in the message data but no one access it.

After some investigation, the most reliable way seems to add some hooks for ```getXXX``` methods and there is indeed some insertion points in C++
```c++
inline ::google::protobuf::int32 Log::test_field() const {
  // @@protoc_insertion_point(...)
  return test_field_;
}
inline void Log::set_test_field(::google::protobuf::int32 value) {
  set_has_test_field();
  test_field_ = value;
  // @@protoc_insertion_point(...)
}
```
However, I don't see these insertion points in java and I also saw a few issues/pull requests previously(#3292, #2684). My questions are:

1. Is there any other ways to do field usage collection and it'd be much helpful if there is some discussions/examples before
2. Whether hooks for ```getXXX``` is feasible or not, I knew java generated sources are much complicated than C++ as there are interfaces/builders/messages inside and I need to modify protoc plugin for my own hook code in Java

Thanks in advance

