I'm looking specifically at
```csharp
private void Merge(
            Node node,
            string path,
            IMessage source,
            IMessage destination,
            FieldMask.MergeOptions options)
```
in `protobuf/csharp/src/Google.Protobuf/FieldMaskTree.cs`, line 248 as of 9f58ee3. (I discovered this behavior while working with an older version.)

What happens when `sourceField` is null but `destinationField` is not?
```csharp
var sourceField = field.Accessor.GetValue(source); // hypothetically null
var destinationField = field.Accessor.GetValue(destination); // hypothetically not null
if (sourceField == null
    && destinationField == null) // false because `destinationField` is not null
{
    continue;
}

if (destinationField == null) // false, but doesn't matter
{
    destinationField = field.MessageType.Parser.CreateTemplate();
    field.Accessor.SetValue(destination, destinationField);
}

var childPath = path.Length == 0 ? entry.Key : path + "." + entry.Key;
Merge(entry.Value, childPath, (IMessage)sourceField, (IMessage)destinationField, options); // called
continue;
```

Unless I misread, `Merge` is recursively called, and in immediately checking `if (source.Descriptor != destination.Descriptor)`, throws a `NullReferenceException`.

Should `FieldMaskTree.Merge` pass `null` into itself while not checking explicitly for `null` arguments?

nodes.body:
```csharp
if (sourceField != null
    || !options.ReplacePrimitiveFields)
{
    field.Accessor.SetValue(destination, sourceField);
}
else
{
    field.Accessor.Clear(destination);
}
```

This merge code has more problems. It's doing null checks where it should be calling `field.Accessor.HasValue()` (especially relevant in the case of primitives), thus resulting in things like set empty strings showing up where there wasn't a set value before. The output of `Merge` should be the same as `IMessage.MergeFrom` if all fields are specified, correct? If so, we also should only be calling `field.Accessor.Clear(destination)` if `options.ReplacePrimitiveFields` is true.

Should be:
```csharp
if ((!field.HasPresence && sourceField != null) || field.Accessor.HasValue(source))
{
    field.Accessor.SetValue(destination, sourceField);
}
else if (options.ReplacePrimitiveFields)
{
    field.Accessor.Clear(destination);
}
```

EDIT: I just realized that I am making extensive use of the `optional` construct in my proto files, and that's where this is failing (`optional string OptionalStringField = 1;`). The descriptors give the right answer, and the protoc codegen will give you Presence fields for them, but stuff like this seems to be broken and none of the tests are covering that use case.

According to [the field_presence.md doc](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/main/docs/field_presence.md#considerations-for-merging), merging should work as expected for all optional fields ever since proto 3.15.

Here is a minimal test showing the second problem that is fixed by the above code suggestion:
```csharp
[Test]
public void MergeOptionalFieldsWithNullFieldsInSource()
{
    var destination = new TestProto3Optional();
    var source = new TestProto3Optional();

    Assert.False(source.HasOptionalString);
    Assert.False(source.HasOptionalSint32);

    Assert.False(destination.HasOptionalString);
    Assert.False(destination.HasOptionalSint32);

    Merge(new FieldMaskTree().AddFieldPath("optional_string").AddFieldPath("optional_sint32"),
        source,
        destination,
        new FieldMask.MergeOptions(),
        false);

    Assert.False(destination.HasOptionalString);
    Assert.False(destination.HasOptionalSint32);
}
```

