The following code will raise `TypeError: Couldn't build proto file into descriptor pool: invalid type for field Timestamp.unit (0)` error if works with `upb` backend, but it works as expected with `cpp`/`python` backends.

```python
from google.protobuf.internal import api_implementation
from google.protobuf.descriptor_pb2 import FileDescriptorProto
from google.protobuf.message_factory import MessageFactory

assert api_implementation.Type() == "upb"

factory = MessageFactory()
f = FileDescriptorProto()
f.ParseFromString(b'\n\rexample.proto"-\n\tTimestamp\x12\r\n\x05value\x18\x01 \x01(\x04\x12\x11\n\x04unit\x18\x02 \x012\x05.Unit*E\n\x04Unit\x12\x0b\n\x07UNKONWN\x10\x00\x12\x0f\n\x0bMILLISECOND\x10\x01\x12\x0f\n\x0bMICROSECOND\x10\x02\x12\x0e\n\nNANOSECOND\x10\x03b\x06proto3')
factory.pool.Add(f)
```

(note: the code is tested with Python protobuf-4.23.0)

The example message is defined as,

```
syntax = "proto3";

enum Unit {
    UNKONWN = 0;
    MILLISECOND = 1;
    MICROSECOND = 2;
    NANOSECOND = 3;
 }  

message Timestamp {
    uint64 value = 1;
    Unit unit = 2;
}
```

The content of `FileDescriptorProto` is as follows,

```
name: "example.proto"
message_type {
  name: "Timestamp"
  field {
    name: "value"
    number: 1
    label: LABEL_OPTIONAL
    type: TYPE_UINT64
  }
  field {
    name: "unit"
    number: 2
    label: LABEL_OPTIONAL
    type_name: ".Unit"
  }
}
enum_type {
  name: "Unit"
  value {
    name: "UNKONWN"
    number: 0
  }
  value {
    name: "MILLISECOND"
    number: 1
  }
  value {
    name: "MICROSECOND"
    number: 2
  }
  value {
    name: "NANOSECOND"
    number: 3
  }
}
syntax: "proto3"
```

Please note, the `unit` field does not have `type`, it only has `type_name`.

According to [link](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/v23.0/src/google/protobuf/descriptor.proto#L230-L232) and [link](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/upb/blob/6c10ce248dbc80da5e3e957803c024be6104fc37/benchmarks/descriptor.proto#L177-L179), the `type` field is not required when `type_name` is set. In fact, it sounds like it recommends to NOT set `type` in that case.

```
  // If type_name is set, this need not be set.  If both this and type_name
  // are set, this must be one of TYPE_ENUM, TYPE_MESSAGE or TYPE_GROUP.
  optional Type type = 5;
```

I know the official protocol compiler generates `FieldDescriptorProto` with both `type` and `type_name` set, but some third-party libraries, e.g., confluent schema registry, do not do that.

Can we update `upb` to handle this case, so it's compatible with `cpp`/`python` backends?

