**What language does this apply to?**

C#

**Describe the problem you are trying to solve.**

When iterating over `MapField`s and `RepeatedField`s using the `GetEnumerator` method, the
enumerator struct is boxed, as the signature of `GetEnumerator` is:

```csharp
public IEnumerator<T> GetEnumerator()
```

The interface return type causes the enumerator to always be boxed. This is not only slower, but
also allocates some memory. The amount of allocated memory is usually not a problem, but if you're
doing it inside of a loop, this might end up being an issue. See the benchmark results at the bottom
of this issue for more details.

**Describe the solution you'd like**

I'd like an API which exposes the enumerator without boxing it. Collections in the BCL also do this,
see
https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/b68382b60349fece947020d234d4a590e9bd5fe8/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Collections/Generic/Dictionary.cs#L365.

Unfortunately it isn't possible to change the return type of `GetEnumerator`, as that method is
required by the `IEnumerable` interface. One option would be to make the implementation explicit, in
which case adding a new `GetEnumerator` method with a different return type becomes possible. This
has the benefit that `foreach` loops will also use this overload, increasing their performance.
However, this would be a breaking change.

An alternative to that might be to simply expose a new method `GetEnumeratorWithoutBoxing`, which
could be consumed manually. For `MapField` this is as simple as exposing the `LinkedList`
enumerator. For `RepeatedField` this would be slightly more involved, as arrays in C# don't have a
struct enumerator, but it would still be a very straight forward implementation. I included the
implementations I used for the benchmarks at the end of this issue.

I'd be more than happy to create a PR for this, if this is something you're interested in.

**Describe alternatives you've considered**

As shown in the benchmarks below, boxing of the `RepeatedField` enumerator can be avoided by using a
`for` loop, which performs comparably to an enumerator which isn't boxed. This workaround is not
possible for the `MapField` type, as you can't index it with an `int`.

**Benchmarks.**

<details>
<summary>Benchmark results MapField</summary>

```

BenchmarkDotNet v0.13.7, Windows 11 (10.0.22621.2134/22H2/2022Update/SunValley2)
11th Gen Intel Core i7-11800H 2.30GHz, 1 CPU, 16 logical and 8 physical cores
.NET SDK 7.0.400
  [Host]             : .NET 7.0.10 (7.0.1023.36312), X64 RyuJIT AVX2
  .NET 7.0           : .NET 7.0.10 (7.0.1023.36312), X64 RyuJIT AVX2
  .NET Framework 4.8 : .NET Framework 4.8.1 (4.8.9167.0), X64 RyuJIT VectorSize=256


```

| Method           | Job                | Runtime            |     Mean |    Error |   StdDev |   Gen0 | Allocated |
| ---------------- | ------------------ | ------------------ | -------: | -------: | -------: | -----: | --------: |
| DefaultForeach   | .NET 7.0           | .NET 7.0           | 53.59 ns | 1.085 ns | 1.372 ns | 0.0038 |      48 B |
| CustomEnumerator | .NET 7.0           | .NET 7.0           | 40.98 ns | 0.606 ns | 0.567 ns |      - |         - |
| DefaultForeach   | .NET Framework 4.8 | .NET Framework 4.8 | 90.82 ns | 1.738 ns | 1.626 ns | 0.0088 |      56 B |
| CustomEnumerator | .NET Framework 4.8 | .NET Framework 4.8 | 43.02 ns | 0.465 ns | 0.435 ns |      - |         - |

</details>

