I like the idea of this - and indeed when writing the initial C# implementation of protobuf back in 2008, I [wrote about wanting static members in interfaces](https://codeblog.jonskeet.uk/2008/08/29/lessons-learned-from-protocol-buffers-part-4-static-interfaces/). (That was about builders rather than parsing, as that version of protobuf used immutable message types and builders, but it's the same idea.)

We'd definitely want a separate interface for this, to avoid it being a breaking change. I'm somewhat nervous about the fact that we'd need to only implement it in some versions. As it happens, we already do that for `IBufferMessage`, so there is at least *some* precedent, although that's done in a slightly different way.

Basically, I'm supportive of the idea - but at the moment I've got so much on that I won't have time to think about the corner cases etc. I'll leave the issue open and *hope* to get to it at some point - but I should warn you that my backlog (particularly of protobuf work as opposed to my main work) is quite long.

In the mean time, if you do create your own interface it should be pretty straightforward to make messages *that you own* implement it due to partial classes - you should be able to just have:

```csharp
public partial class Message1 : IParseableMessage {}
public partial class Message2 : IParseableMessage {}
public partial class Message3 : IParseableMessage {}
public partial class Message4 : IParseableMessage {}
```

... and let it just use the declarations from the "main" generated code. You could then have a unit test to make sure all the messages are covered. That won't help for any protos you're not generating yourself, of course.

Sorry that this probably isn't quite as "yeah, let's go!" as you'd have liked - I wish I could clone myself to get to everything I want to do :(

@davidkarlsson: `TimeOnly` just doesn't have a logical representation in protobuf. Sure, you *can* convert a `TimeOnly` to a `TimeSpan`, but they're logically not the same thing. A "time of day" and "elapsed number of milliseconds" are *not* the same, and in my experience trying to accept them as if they're interchangeable is a recipe for confusion.

A time of day is *not* a "time elapsed since midnight", in general - even if it is most of the time.

If you want to add your own extension methods and factory methods in your own code, you can do so - but I would strongly resist putting it into Google.Protobuf.

