**What version of protobuf and what language are you using?**
Version: 3.19.4

```
> protoc --version
libprotoc 3.19.4
```

Language: Java

**What operating system (Linux, Windows, ...) and version?**
macOS 14.2.1 (23C71)

**What runtime / compiler are you using (e.g., python version or gcc version)**
11.0.13-msft

**What did you do?**
When a message has a field that is an enum, two getters and setters are generated -- one for the enum type and another for the enum's value which accepts an `int` and has the suffix "Value" (e.g. `MyEnum getFoo()` and `int getFooValue()`). This can lead to generating invalid Java code if another field in the message has the same name as the enum field + "Value".

Define an enum and message:
```proto
syntax = "proto3";

package example;

option java_multiple_files = true;
option java_package = "com.example";
option java_outer_classname = "DoubleGettingExamples";

enum Property {
  Property_FOO = 0;
  Property_BAR = 1;
  Property_BAZ = 2;
}

message DoubleGettingExample {
  Property example = 1;
  string exampleValue = 2; // string example_value = 2; also has this problem because the codegen is the same.
}
```

Generate Java code:
```
protoc -I=/Users/me/example --java_out=/Users/me/example /Users/me/example/DoubleGetterExample.proto 
```

Resulting Java class has two different `getExampleValue` methods that differ in return type, which doesn't compile:
```java
public static final int EXAMPLE_FIELD_NUMBER = 1;
private int example_ = 0;
/**
 * <code>.example.Property example = 1;</code>
 * @return The enum numeric value on the wire for example.
 */
@java.lang.Override public int getExampleValue() {
  return example_;
}
/**
 * <code>.example.Property example = 1;</code>
 * @return The example.
 */
@java.lang.Override public com.linkedin.example.Property getExample() {
  com.linkedin.example.Property result = com.linkedin.example.Property.forNumber(example_);
  return result == null ? com.linkedin.example.Property.UNRECOGNIZED : result;
}

public static final int EXAMPLEVALUE_FIELD_NUMBER = 2;
@SuppressWarnings("serial")
private volatile java.lang.Object exampleValue_ = "";
/**
 * <code>string exampleValue = 2;</code>
 * @return The exampleValue.
 */
@java.lang.Override
public java.lang.String getExampleValue() {
  java.lang.Object ref = exampleValue_;
  if (ref instanceof java.lang.String) {
    return (java.lang.String) ref;
  } else {
    com.google.protobuf.ByteString bs = 
        (com.google.protobuf.ByteString) ref;
    java.lang.String s = bs.toStringUtf8();
    exampleValue_ = s;
    return s;
  }
}
```

Steps to reproduce the behavior:
1. Define an enum and a message.
2. Use the enum in the message.
3. Add another field to the message that has the same name as the enum field + `Value` at the end. The pattern of enum field + `_value` also has this problem.
4. Compile the `proto` to Java.
5. Observe that multiple getters with the same name are generated.

**What did you expect to see**
I was hoping that a valid `proto` will result in the generation of valid Java source code. In some field name collision cases, `protoc` can handle it by renaming the generated getters/setters, but in this case it seems like it doesn't.

**What did you see instead?**
The resulting Java code is not valid since it generates a class with duplicate methods that differ in their return type, which is not allowed by the Java compiler.

**Anything else we should know about your project / environment**
We are currently working on a project to automatically convert data models from another format to `proto`, but sometimes the original fields have this kind of pattern, which breaks the resulting code. As a result, we have to work around this by mangling the perfectly good names of fields during conversion to `proto`.

