In protobuf for golang, there is no semantic difference between being explicitly set to `nil` or it simply not appearing, as there is no semantic concept of “nil” in protobuf. The proto wire only allows a field to be either unpopulated, or set to a valid value. This follows into the JSON mapping, where `null` is equivalent to the field being not present.

The documentation does indeed say that presence can be checked by using `has_foo()`, however, that is for other languages. The [example code shown for Golang](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/issues) make it clear:
```go
m := GetProto()
if m.Foo != nil {
  // Clear the field:
  m.Foo = nil
} else {
  // Field is not present, so set it.
  m.Foo = proto.Int32(1)
}
```

