File: [src/google/protobuf/compiler/subprocess.cc](https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/blob/dbf7adfabc123e6efca12135bac8c5db07ff8114/src/google/protobuf/compiler/subprocess.cc)

Function: Subprocess::Start

Details:

In the `Subprocess::Start` function, `argv[0]` is initialized using the `portable_strdup` function.

The `portable_strdup` function takes into account the possibility of `malloc` returning `nullptr` for some reason; in such cases, `portable_strdup` will also return `nullptr`.

If there is a situation where `argv[0]` is initialized to `nullptr` by the `portable_strdup` function, and then subsequently passed unchecked as a parameter to the `execvp` and `execv` functions, both of which are annotated with the `nonnull` attribute for their 1st parameter, this leads to undefined behavior.

For your convenience, I have copied and pasted the relevant code as follow:

```C++
char* portable_strdup(const char* s) {
  char* ns = (char*)malloc(strlen(s) + 1);
  if (ns != nullptr) { // Assuming `ns` is `nullptr`, skip this branch.
    strcpy(ns, s);
  }
  return ns;	// return `nullptr`
}

void Subprocess::Start(const std::string& program, SearchMode search_mode) {
	...
    // `argv[0]` is initialized to `nullptr`
    char* argv[2] = {portable_strdup(program.c_str()), nullptr};
    ...
    if (child_pid_ == -1) {
        ...
    } else if (child_pid_ == 0) {
    	...
        switch (search_mode) {
          case SEARCH_PATH:
            // `nullptr` passed to 1st parameter excepting 'nonnull'
            execvp(argv[0], argv);
            break;
          case EXACT_NAME:
            execv(argv[0], argv);
            break;
        }
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

