nodes.body:
If I'm understanding the issue correct, unfortunately I think you just inherently can't do what you're asking about

With protobuf, java-lite versus java-full has a number of places where the APIs are _source_ compatible but not _jar/bytecode_ compatible, so if you compile against java-lite the resulting jar can't be swapped out to use java-full or vice versa.

To clarify the difference between the source compatible and bytecode compatible, consider a case like

```
class Base {}
class Sub { int x() { return 0; } }
```

Or:

```
class BaseClass { int x() { return 0; } }
class Sub extends Base { }
```

And caller code has been written as:
```
Sub s = new Sub();
s.x();
```

That 'caller code' is source compatible with both of the implementations, where the java syntax allows s.x() either way. But when you compile it to a .class file. javac will actually resolve and statically emit it as directly either a Base::x() or a Sub::x() call. So the two definitions of Base and Sub both can be compiled from source with that snippet, but if you compile from source with one of them and then swap out the implementation after and use a mismatched .class it will have in the .class file a reference to something that isn't there.

I think you'll unfortunately need to compile the two libraries from source with the same java protobuf version, _or_ resort to much more complicated tricks to get 2 copies of protobuf with different packages so you can ship both without a collision.

